// English text for the concept cards (titles, hooks, bodies, deep notes, symbol readings, quizzes). Same ids and order as cards.js.
export const CARD_EN = {
  twobody: { title: 'The two-body problem', tag: 'Where orbital mechanics begins',
    body: `When there are only two bodies and the only force is their mutual gravity, we call it the two-body problem. It is the first thing to learn because it is the starting point for orbit calculations, such as a planet and the Sun or a satellite and Earth. In this case the orbit of the lighter body around the heavier one is always a <b>conic section</b> (circle, ellipse, parabola or hyperbola), and which one it is depends only on the launch speed. For example, throw it at circular-orbit speed and you get a circle; faster and you get an ellipse; at √2 times circular speed (escape speed) it follows a parabola and never comes back.
      Below, change the sideways launch speed from the same spot (distance r₀).`,
    deep: String.raw`Because the angular momentum <span class="tex">\vec h = \vec r \times \dot{\vec r}</span> is conserved, the motion stays in one plane. Crossing the equation of motion with <span class="tex">\vec h</span> and integrating shows that the Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector is constant, and from it comes the orbit equation
      <span class="tex block">r = \frac{h^2/\mu}{1 + e\cos\nu}</span>. Here <span class="tex">\mu = G(m_1+m_2)</span> and ν is the true anomaly.`,
    s: ['position vector seen from the central body', 'acceleration (position differentiated twice in time)', 'gravitational parameter GM (proportional to the central mass)', 'distance'],
    r: 'The body is always accelerated toward the center (the minus sign), with a strength inversely proportional to the square of the distance. This simple law alone produces circular, elliptical, parabolic and hyperbolic orbits.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'If you throw something √2 times faster than circular-orbit speed, what orbit do you get?', opts: ['A bigger circle', 'An ellipse', 'A parabola (escape)', 'It falls'], why: '√2 times circular speed is escape speed, so the path is a parabola.' },
      { q: 'What force is considered in the two-body problem?', opts: ['Only the gravity between the two bodies', 'Gravity and air resistance', 'The gravity of many planets'], why: 'The two-body problem deals with just two bodies acting on each other only through gravity.' },
      { q: 'In the two-body problem, what decides whether the orbit is a circle, an ellipse or a parabola?', opts: ['The color of the object', 'The launch speed', 'The shape of the object'], why: 'Thrown from the same spot, the kind of orbit is decided by the launch speed alone.' },
      { q: 'If the distance from the center doubles, how does the acceleration change?', opts: ['1/2 as much', '1/4 as much', '2 times'], why: 'Acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of distance, so doubling the distance gives 1/4.' },
    ] },
  invariants: { title: 'Conserved quantities: energy, angular momentum, eccentricity vector', tag: 'What stays the same while it moves',
    body: `While a spacecraft orbits, its speed and distance keep changing, but three quantities stay fixed: the <b>specific energy ε</b>, the <b>specific angular momentum h</b>, and the <b>eccentricity vector e</b> that points to periapsis. They do not change as long as only gravity acts. So if you remember an orbit by these three values instead of the instantaneous position and velocity, you know its size, shape and orientation at once, and every calculation becomes easier. For example, the specific energy is set by the semi-major axis alone, so you can see right away that a larger orbit is less tightly bound.`,
    deep: String.raw`<span class="tex">\vec e</span> points toward periapsis and its length is the eccentricity. <span class="tex">h^2 = \mu a(1-e^2)</span>, <span class="tex">r_p = a(1-e)</span>, <span class="tex">r_a = a(1+e)</span>.
      In real orbits these values change slowly because of J2, atmospheric drag and third-body perturbations; the study of their rates of change is perturbation theory (the Lagrange and Gauss planetary equations).`,
    s: ['specific energy: kinetic plus potential energy per unit mass', 'speed', 'gravitational parameter GM', 'distance', 'semi-major axis', 'eccentricity vector: points to periapsis, its length is the eccentricity', 'specific angular momentum r × v'],
    r: 'Even as the body speeds up and slows down along the orbit, ε stays the same. If ε is negative the orbit is a bound ellipse, and its value sets the orbit size a. The e vector tells you in which direction the orbit is stretched.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'The specific energy of an elliptical orbit is ε = −μ/(2a). If the semi-major axis doubles, what happens to ε?', opts: ['It doubles', 'It halves (closer to 0)', 'It stays the same', 'It quadruples'], why: 'Since ε ∝ −1/a, it halves and moves closer to 0 (less tightly bound).' },
      { q: 'Under gravity alone, which of these is NOT conserved during an orbit?', opts: ['Specific energy ε', 'Specific angular momentum h', 'The instantaneous speed'], why: 'Speed and distance keep changing, but ε, h and the eccentricity vector e stay fixed.' },
      { q: 'What kind of orbit has a negative specific energy ε?', opts: ['A bound elliptical orbit', 'A parabolic orbit', 'A hyperbolic orbit'], why: 'A negative ε means the body is bound in an elliptical orbit.' },
      { q: 'What does the eccentricity vector e tell you?', opts: ['The orbital period', 'The direction in which the orbit is stretched (toward periapsis)', 'The mass of the body'], why: 'The e vector points to periapsis, telling you which way the orbit is stretched.' },
    ] },
  elements: { title: 'The six classical orbital elements', tag: 'An orbit in six numbers',
    body: `How many numbers do you need to describe an orbit exactly? The answer is six, called the classical orbital elements. An orbit in space is fixed only when you know its shape, tilt, orientation and the current position, so satellite orbits are recorded with these six values. The shape is set by the semi-major axis a and the eccentricity e, the tilt by the inclination i, the orientation by the longitude of the ascending node Ω and the argument of periapsis ω, and the current position by the true anomaly ν. The inclination alone tells you a lot: i = 0° is an equatorial orbit, 90° is a polar orbit, and above 90° the orbit is retrograde, moving opposite to Earth's rotation. In the figure below the gray circle is the reference plane (the equator or the ecliptic); move the sliders to see how each element changes the orbit.`,
    deep: String.raw`The transformation from the perifocal frame to the inertial frame is <span class="tex">R = R_3(-\Omega)\,R_1(-i)\,R_3(-\omega)</span>. For circular orbits (e = 0) or equatorial orbits (i = 0), ω or Ω is undefined, so substitutes such as the argument of latitude or the true longitude are used. Numerical work sometimes uses the singularity-free equinoctial elements.`,
    s: ['semi-major axis: the size of the orbit', 'eccentricity: how stretched it is (0 = circle)', 'inclination: the tilt relative to the reference plane', 'longitude of the ascending node: direction of the point where the orbit crosses the reference plane going upward', 'argument of periapsis: angle from the ascending node to periapsis', 'true anomaly: angle from periapsis to the current position'],
    r: 'The first two give the shape of the orbit, the next three its orientation in space, and the last one where on the orbit the body is now. With these six numbers the position and velocity are completely known.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What is an orbit with inclination i greater than 90°?', opts: ['A prograde orbit', 'A retrograde orbit', 'An equatorial orbit', 'A polar orbit'], why: 'Above 90° the orbit goes opposite to the rotation, a retrograde orbit. A sun-synchronous orbit (about 98°) is an example.' },
      { q: 'How many classical orbital elements are there?', unit: '', why: 'To describe an orbit exactly you need six values: a, e, i, Ω, ω and ν.' },
      { q: 'Which two elements set the shape of the orbit?', opts: ['Inclination i and longitude of the ascending node Ω', 'Semi-major axis a and eccentricity e', 'Argument of periapsis ω and true anomaly ν'], why: 'The shape is set by the semi-major axis a and the eccentricity e.' },
      { q: 'What is an orbit with inclination i = 90° called?', opts: ['An equatorial orbit', 'A polar orbit', 'A retrograde orbit'], why: 'i = 0° is equatorial, 90° is polar, and above 90° is retrograde.' },
    ] },
  kepler: { title: 'Kepler\'s equation', tag: 'Given a time, where is it?',
    body: `Kepler's equation tells you where a body on an elliptical orbit will be after some time. It links the mean anomaly M, which grows evenly with time, to the eccentric anomaly E, which describes the actual position. The conversion is needed because a body on an elliptical orbit does not move evenly: it moves fast near periapsis and slowly near apoapsis. Getting E from M cannot be done algebraically, so the answer is approximated by repeated steps of Newton's method. On a circular orbit with zero eccentricity, however, the body moves evenly and E equals M.`,
    deep: String.raw`Newton's method: <span class="tex">E_{k+1} = E_k - \frac{E_k - e\sin E_k - M}{1 - e\cos E_k}</span>. The starting value is usually <span class="tex">E_0 = M</span>; for large eccentricity <span class="tex">E_0=\pi</span> is safer. Then
      <span class="tex">\tan\frac{\nu}{2} = \sqrt{\frac{1+e}{1-e}}\tan\frac{E}{2}</span>. For hyperbolic orbits <span class="tex">M = e\sinh H - H</span>; to handle all conics with one formula, universal variables (Stumpff functions) are used.`,
    s: ['mean anomaly: an angle that grows evenly with time', 'eccentric anomaly: an auxiliary angle used to compute the position', 'eccentricity', 'mean motion: angular speed, 360° divided by the period', 'time of periapsis passage'],
    r: 'Given a time, first find M, solve this equation for E, then get the actual position from E. If e is 0 then E = M; the larger e is, the faster the position changes near periapsis.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'If the eccentricity is 0, how are E and M related?', opts: ['E = M', 'E = 2M', 'E = M/2', 'E = 0'], why: 'With e = 0, M = E − 0, so they are equal (motion is even on a circular orbit).' },
      { q: 'Where does a body on an elliptical orbit move fastest?', opts: ['Near periapsis', 'Near apoapsis', 'The same everywhere'], why: 'The body moves fast near periapsis and slowly near apoapsis.' },
      { q: 'Which angle grows evenly in proportion to time?', opts: ['Eccentric anomaly E', 'Mean anomaly M', 'Inclination i'], why: 'The mean anomaly M grows evenly with time.' },
      { q: 'How is E found from M?', opts: ['A closed-form formula', 'Repeated approximation such as Newton\'s method', 'Eyeballing a graph'], why: 'Kepler\'s equation cannot be solved algebraically, so it is approximated by repeated Newton steps.' },
    ] },
  hohmann: { title: 'Hohmann and bi-elliptic transfers', tag: 'Is Hohmann always the cheapest?',
    body: `A Hohmann transfer is an elliptical orbit that moves a spacecraft between two circular orbits with two burns. One burn on the starting orbit puts it on the transfer ellipse, and another burn at the target orbit makes it circular. This usually uses the least fuel, so it is widely used for planetary missions and for reaching geostationary orbit. But if the outer orbit is more than about 12 times the inner one (and always above 15.6 times), a <b>bi-elliptic transfer</b> (three burns) that deliberately goes farther out and comes back costs less. Remember, though, that a bi-elliptic transfer takes much longer.`,
    deep: String.raw`For R > 15.58 the bi-elliptic transfer wins regardless of the intermediate radius r_b; for 11.94 < R < 15.58 it wins only when r_b is large enough. The limit r_b → ∞ is the bi-parabolic transfer, which escapes and changes direction for free at infinity, with <span class="tex">\Delta v = (\sqrt2-1)(1+1/\sqrt R)\sqrt{\mu/r_1}</span>.
      The key is the Oberth effect: a burn deep in a gravity well changes the energy more.`,
    s: ['total velocity change for a Hohmann transfer', 'gravitational parameter of the central body', 'radii of the starting and target circular orbits', 'radius ratio r₂/r₁'],
    r: 'The first term is the burn from the starting orbit onto the transfer ellipse; the second is the burn that circularizes at arrival. It is the cheapest two-burn way to move between two circular orbits.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'How many burns does a Hohmann transfer use?', unit: 'burns', why: 'One on the starting orbit and one on the target orbit.' },
      { q: 'A Hohmann transfer moves a spacecraft between what?', opts: ['Two circular orbits', 'The surfaces of two planets', 'Two hyperbolic orbits'], why: 'A Hohmann transfer is an elliptical orbit that moves between two circular orbits with two burns.' },
      { q: 'How many burns does a bi-elliptic transfer use?', unit: 'burns', why: 'It deliberately goes farther out and comes back, so it uses three burns.' },
      { q: 'When the outer orbit is more than 15.6 times the inner one, which describes a bi-elliptic transfer?', opts: ['More fuel but faster', 'Less fuel but much longer', 'More fuel and more time'], why: 'In that case a bi-elliptic transfer uses less fuel but takes much longer.' },
    ] },
  plane: { title: 'Changing the orbital plane', tag: 'Just turning costs a lot',
    body: `Tilting an orbit, a plane change, is more expensive than you might think. To tilt the orbital plane you must turn the direction of the velocity rather than its size, so the faster you are moving, the more fuel it takes. For example, changing the plane of a low Earth orbit (7.8 km/s) by 60° needs a Δv of 7.8 km/s, about the cost of launching a new satellite. So plane changes are usually done at apoapsis where the speed is low, and <b>combined</b> with other burns.`,
    deep: String.raw`Changing size and direction in one burn follows the law of cosines, <span class="tex">\Delta v = \sqrt{v_1^2+v_2^2-2v_1v_2\cos\Delta i}</span>. A geostationary satellite circularizes and removes the plane tilt together at the apoapsis of a transfer orbit inclined by the launch site latitude (Naro 34.4°, Cape Canaveral 28.5°). This is why launch sites near the equator have an advantage.`,
    s: ['required velocity change', 'orbital speed at the moment of the burn', 'change in inclination'],
    r: 'This is the cost of turning the velocity by Δi while keeping its size. It is proportional to v, so changing at the slow-moving apoapsis is cheaper. Changing by 60° costs as much as the current speed.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'A satellite moving at 7.8 km/s changes its orbital plane by 60°. What is the Δv in km/s?', unit: 'km/s', why: '2v sin(30°) = v = 7.8 km/s, about the same as a new launch.' },
      { q: 'In a plane change, what part of the velocity is changed?', opts: ['Its size', 'Its direction', 'Size and direction equally'], why: 'Tilting the orbital plane requires turning the direction of the velocity, not its size.' },
      { q: 'Where is the best place to do a plane change?', opts: ['At periapsis, where the speed is highest', 'At apoapsis, where the speed is low', 'Anywhere is the same'], why: 'The cost is proportional to the speed v, so it is cheaper at the slow apoapsis.' },
      { q: 'For the same plane-change angle, if the satellite speed is halved, the needed Δv is', opts: ['the same', 'halved', 'doubled'], why: 'The plane-change cost is proportional to v, so half the speed means half the Δv.' },
    ] },
  oberth: { title: 'The Oberth effect', tag: 'Step on the gas when you are fast',
    body: `The Oberth effect is the fact that the same Δv gives more energy when the burn is made while moving fast. That is why spacecraft fire their engines close to a planet, where they are fastest. The change in kinetic energy is proportional to <b>v·Δv</b>, so adding the same Δv where the speed v is already large increases the energy more. So in an orbit it is better to burn at perihelion than at aphelion. Below, compare how the leftover speed after escape, v∞, depends on the altitude of the burn.`,
    deep: String.raw`<span class="tex">\Delta\varepsilon = v\,\Delta v + \tfrac12\Delta v^2</span>. The kinetic energy carried by the propellant itself is used too, so there is no contradiction with energy conservation. Ideas such as a powered flyby, burning at periapsis, or an 'Oberth maneuver' burning near the Sun to greatly raise escape speed have been studied.`,
    s: ['speed left after leaving the sphere of gravity', 'speed at the burn point', 'speed added by the burn', 'escape speed at that point'],
    r: 'v∞² is the kinetic energy after the burn minus the energy needed to escape. Burning when v_p is large (at periapsis) makes (v_p + Δv)² grow a lot, so the same Δv gives a larger v∞.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'For the same Δv, where should you burn to gain the most energy?', opts: ['Aphelion', 'Perihelion', 'Anywhere is the same', 'Outside the orbit'], why: 'Burning when fastest (at perihelion) makes v·Δv larger.' },
      { q: 'To use the Oberth effect, when should the engine be fired?', opts: ['When slowest', 'When fastest (close to the planet)', 'It does not matter'], why: 'The same Δv gives more energy when the burn is made while moving fast.' },
      { q: 'The change in kinetic energy is proportional to what?', opts: ['v·Δv', 'Δv only', 'v only'], why: 'The change in kinetic energy is proportional to v·Δv, so the effect is larger where v is large.' },
      { q: 'To make the leftover speed v∞ after escape as large as possible, where should the same Δv be spent?', opts: ['At apoapsis', 'At periapsis (where v_p is large)', 'At the edge of the sphere of influence'], why: 'Burning when v_p is large makes (v_p + Δv)² grow a lot, giving a larger v∞.' },
    ] },
  rocket: { title: 'The rocket equation', tag: 'Why a rocket is mostly fuel',
    body: `The velocity change Δv a rocket can achieve is set by the rocket equation. A rocket carries heavy propellant and pushes itself forward by throwing it backward, so the velocity change is proportional to the exhaust speed (specific impulse I<sub>sp</sub>) and to the <b>logarithm</b> of the mass ratio. As a formula, Δv = I<sub>sp</sub> × g₀ × ln(initial mass ÷ final mass), with g₀ = 9.81 m/s². For example, if the mass ratio is e (about 2.72, ln = 1), Δv is I<sub>sp</sub> × 9.81 m/s. Reaching low Earth orbit takes about 9.4 km/s (including drag and gravity losses), so a kerosene engine needs a mass ratio above 20. This is why rockets are split into stages that drop empty tanks on the way up.`,
    deep: String.raw`Integrate momentum conservation <span class="tex">m\,dv = -v_e\,dm</span> (Tsiolkovsky, 1903). Staging discards used tanks and improves the remaining mass ratio. Ion engines have I_sp above 3,000 s, but their thrust is tiny, so they accelerate over months.`,
    s: ['velocity change the rocket can achieve', 'specific impulse: the fuel efficiency of the engine (seconds)', 'standard gravity 9.81 m/s²', 'initial mass (including propellant)', 'mass after the propellant is used up'],
    r: 'I_sp × g₀ is the speed of the exhaust gas. Carrying twice the propellant does not double Δv; it adds only ln 2 (about 0.69) times as much. That is why rockets are mostly fuel and use stages.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'A rocket with I_sp = 300 s has a mass ratio of e (about 2.72). What is Δv, in km/s?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'Δv = I_sp g₀ ln(e) = 300 × 9.81 ≈ 2.94 km/s.' },
      { q: 'Why are rockets built in stages?', opts: ['To drop empty tanks and get lighter', 'To look nicer', 'To increase air resistance'], why: 'Dropping empty tanks on the way up gives a larger Δv from the same propellant.' },
      { q: 'If you double the mass ratio by carrying more propellant, what happens to Δv?', opts: ['It doubles', 'It increases only by ln 2 (about 0.69) times the factor', 'It quadruples'], why: 'Δv is proportional to the logarithm of the mass ratio, so it grows by only ln 2, not twice.' },
      { q: 'About how many km/s of Δv are needed to reach low Earth orbit?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'Including drag and gravity losses, about 9.4 km/s is needed.' },
    ] },
  soi: { title: 'Sphere of influence and patched conics', tag: 'Whose gravity to follow',
    body: `Near a planet a spacecraft feels mainly the planet's gravity; far away, mainly the Sun's. The boundary is the <b>sphere of influence</b>, and the calculation can be split there. So an interplanetary trip is computed by joining pieces, an orbit around the Sun plus hyperbolas around the planets; this is the patched-conic approximation. For example, the Moon (about 380,000 km away) is inside Earth's sphere of influence (about 920,000 km), so its orbit is computed around Earth.`,
    deep: String.raw`Laplace's definition: the distance at which the ratio of perturbing to main gravity is the same in the two descriptions (Sun-centered plus planet perturbation, and planet-centered plus Sun perturbation). The Hill radius <span class="tex">r_H = a(m/3M)^{1/3}</span> is the range in which a moon can stay stably, and it is larger than the sphere of influence. In the sandbox flyby exercise, starting at the edge of the sphere of influence lets you see the Sun's tidal force make the result differ from the formula.`,
    s: ['sphere-of-influence radius: inside it the planet\'s gravity dominates', 'orbital radius of the planet', 'mass of the planet', 'mass of the Sun'],
    r: 'The farther a planet is from the Sun and the heavier it is, the larger its sphere of influence. Because it goes as the 2/5 power of the mass ratio, Jupiter (1/1047 of the Sun) has a sphere of influence about 6% of its orbital radius.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Is the Moon inside Earth\'s sphere of influence (about 920,000 km)?', opts: ['Inside', 'Outside'], why: 'The Moon is 380,000 km away, inside the sphere of influence, so it can be computed around Earth.' },
      { q: 'When a spacecraft is far from a planet, whose gravity does it mainly feel?', opts: ['The planet\'s', 'The Sun\'s', 'The Moon\'s'], why: 'Near a planet it feels mainly the planet\'s gravity, and far away mainly the Sun\'s.' },
      { q: 'How does the patched-conic approximation compute an interplanetary trip?', opts: ['By computing the gravity of all bodies at once', 'By joining a Sun-centered orbit and planet-centered hyperbola pieces', 'As straight-line motion'], why: 'It splits the calculation at the sphere-of-influence boundary and joins conic pieces.' },
      { q: 'Which makes a sphere of influence larger?', opts: ['A light planet close to the Sun', 'A heavy planet far from the Sun', 'A fast-spinning planet'], why: 'The farther from the Sun and the heavier the planet, the larger its sphere of influence.' },
    ] },
  flyby: { title: 'Gravity assist and turn angle', tag: 'Borrowing speed from a planet',
    body: `A gravity assist is a way for a spacecraft to change its speed and direction without fuel by swinging past a planet. Seen from the planet, the spacecraft leaves at the same speed it arrived with, only turned by the angle δ. But its velocity relative to the Sun is this plus the planet's velocity, so when the direction changes, the size changes too. This is how a spacecraft can gain or lose speed from a planet. Notice that the turn angle is larger the closer and slower the pass.`,
    deep: String.raw`Aiming distance <span class="tex">b = r_p\sqrt{1 + 2\mu/(r_p v_\infty^2)}</span>. Real missions correct the trajectory to hit an aim point <span class="tex">\vec B = (B\cdot T, B\cdot R)</span> in the B-plane (the plane through the planet's center perpendicular to v∞). The maximum velocity change is <span class="tex">2v_\infty\sin(\delta/2)</span>.`,
    s: ['turn angle: how much the direction changes passing the planet', 'closest approach distance (from the planet\'s center)', 'approach speed relative to the planet', 'gravitational parameter of the planet'],
    r: 'The closer (small r_p) and slower (small v∞) the pass, the closer 1 + r_p v∞²/μ gets to 1 and the larger the turn angle. The heavier the planet (larger μ), the more it bends.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'In a flyby, how does the planet-relative speed v∞ compare on the way in and out?', opts: ['It increases', 'It decreases', 'It is the same'], why: 'Relative to the planet, energy is conserved, so it is the same; only the direction changes. The Sun-relative velocity changes.' },
      { q: 'How much fuel is needed to change speed and direction with a gravity assist?', opts: ['A lot', 'None', 'In proportion to the planet\'s size'], why: 'A gravity assist changes speed and direction without fuel, by swinging past a planet.' },
      { q: 'Why can the size of the spacecraft\'s Sun-relative velocity change?', opts: ['Because it is the planet-relative velocity plus the planet\'s velocity', 'Because the planet gives it fuel', 'Because the solar wind pushes it'], why: 'Relative to the planet only the direction changes, but adding the planet\'s velocity, the Sun-relative size changes too.' },
      { q: 'When does the turn angle δ get larger?', opts: ['When passing far and fast', 'When passing close and slow', 'When passing a light planet'], why: 'The smaller r_p and v∞ and the heavier the planet, the larger the turn angle.' },
    ] },
  lambert: { title: 'Lambert\'s problem', tag: 'Two points and a time give one orbit',
    body: `"To leave Earth on this date and reach Mars on that date, which orbit should we take?" This question, which must be solved when planning a spacecraft launch, is Lambert's problem. Fix the departure point, the arrival point and the flight time (going the same way within one revolution), and the orbit is determined, giving the required departure velocity as the answer. Below, change the angle between the two points and the flight time.`,
    deep: String.raw`Using the universal variable z (positive for ellipses, negative for hyperbolas) and the Stumpff functions C(z), S(z), it can be solved with one formula regardless of the conic type (Curtis, chapter 5). <span class="tex">A = \sin\Delta\theta\sqrt{r_1r_2/(1-\cos\Delta\theta)}</span>. When the transfer angle is near 180°, A → 0 and the orbital plane becomes undefined, a singularity. A porkchop plot is the result of solving this problem thousands of times.`,
    s: ['gravitational parameter of the central body', 'flight time', 'a variable describing the orbit shape (positive ellipse, negative hyperbola)', 'auxiliary values set by the two positions and the transfer angle', 'Stumpff functions: generalized trigonometric functions valid for any conic type'],
    r: 'With the two positions fixed, the orbit matching the flight time Δt can be found by varying z alone. A computer finds the z satisfying this equation by iteration and gets the departure velocity from it.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which of these is NOT given in Lambert\'s problem?', opts: ['Departure position', 'Arrival position', 'Flight time', 'Departure velocity'], why: 'The departure velocity is the answer being sought.' },
      { q: 'What does Lambert\'s problem give as its answer?', opts: ['The required departure velocity', 'The mass of the planet', 'The type of spacecraft fuel'], why: 'Once the orbit is fixed, the required departure velocity on it is the answer.' },
      { q: 'When does Lambert\'s problem mainly need to be solved?', opts: ['When planning a spacecraft launch', 'When measuring a star\'s temperature', 'When predicting tides'], why: 'It is the core launch-planning question of which orbit leaves on one date and arrives on another.' },
      { q: 'How does a computer find the orbit that matches the flight time Δt?', opts: ['By varying z with repeated calculation', 'By picking one at random', 'By approximating with a straight path'], why: 'With the two positions fixed, it varies z alone and finds the solution matching Δt by iteration.' },
    ] },
  j2: { title: 'J2 perturbation and sun-synchronous orbits', tag: 'A squashed Earth turns the orbit',
    body: `Earth is not a perfect sphere; its equator bulges (J2 = 1.0826×10⁻³), so satellite orbital planes slowly rotate. This is the J2 perturbation. If this rotation is set to one turn per year (0.9856° per day), the orbital plane keeps the same angle to the Sun, giving a <b>sun-synchronous orbit</b>. For this, a low orbit needs an inclination of about 98° (slightly retrograde) so the plane turns 0.9856° east per day. This is why Earth-observation satellites such as KOMPSAT and the CAS500 series pass over at the same local time every day.`,
    deep: String.raw`J2 is the largest non-spherical term in the spherical-harmonic expansion of Earth's gravitational potential. The argument of periapsis also rotates, <span class="tex">\dot\omega = \tfrac34 nJ_2(R/p)^2(5\cos^2 i - 1)</span>, and stops at i = 63.4°. That is why Molniya orbits use this inclination.`,
    s: ['rate at which the orbital plane turns (motion of the ascending node)', 'mean motion (orbital angular speed)', 'coefficient for Earth\'s flattening, 1.08×10⁻³', 'Earth\'s equatorial radius', 'semi-latus rectum of the orbit, a(1−e²)', 'inclination'],
    r: 'The bulge at Earth\'s equator pulls on the orbit and slowly turns its plane. Because of cos i, prograde orbits (i < 90°) turn west and retrograde ones (i > 90°) turn east. Choosing i so it turns 0.9856° east per day gives a sun-synchronous orbit.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What is the approximate inclination of a sun-synchronous orbit?', opts: ['About 28°', 'About 63°', 'About 98°', '0°'], why: 'In low orbit it must be a retrograde orbit of about 97-99° for the plane to turn once a year.' },
      { q: 'What causes the J2 perturbation?', opts: ['Earth\'s bulging equator', 'The Moon\'s orbit', 'The solar wind'], why: 'Earth is not a perfect sphere; its bulging equator slowly turns satellite orbital planes.' },
      { q: 'For a sun-synchronous orbit, how many degrees per day must the plane turn?', unit: '°', why: 'One turn per year, that is 0.9856° per day toward the east.' },
      { q: 'Which way does J2 turn the plane of a prograde orbit (i < 90°)?', opts: ['East', 'West', 'It does not turn'], why: 'Because of cos i, prograde orbits turn west and retrograde orbits turn east.' },
    ] },
  cr3bp: { title: 'The restricted three-body problem and the Jacobi constant', tag: 'Where you can and cannot go',
    body: `The circular restricted three-body problem studies how a very light third object moves near two bodies orbiting each other. A spacecraft traveling between Earth and the Moon is a typical example. In a frame rotating with Earth and the Moon, the <b>Jacobi constant C</b> is conserved instead of energy, and it decides where the spacecraft can go. If C is large (low energy), the spacecraft is trapped around Earth or around the Moon; lower C and the L1 neck opens, so it can cross to the Moon. The dark regions in the figure are where it cannot go for that C. Of the five Lagrange points, L1, L2 and L3 are unstable, and only L4 and L5 at the corners of equilateral triangles are stable, so objects can gather there, like the Trojan asteroids on Jupiter's orbit.`,
    deep: String.raw`Earth-Moon mass ratio μ = 0.01215. C at the Lagrange points: L1 3.188, L2 3.172, L3 3.012, L4 and L5 2.988. Between these values the topology of the zero-velocity curves changes. Danuri reached the Moon on a ballistic lunar transfer (BLT) that went out near the Sun-Earth L1 point and back to save fuel, an example of using this structure.`,
    s: ['Jacobi constant: the quantity conserved in the rotating frame', 'effective potential: gravity and centrifugal force combined', 'speed in the rotating frame', 'mass fraction of the smaller body (the Moon)', 'distances to the two bodies'],
    r: 'Since v² = 2Ω − C, wherever 2Ω < C the value of v² would be negative, so those places cannot be reached. The larger C (the lower the energy), the wider the forbidden region.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which Lagrange points are stable?', opts: ['L1 and L2', 'L3', 'L4 and L5', 'All of them'], why: 'L4 and L5 are stable if the mass ratio is small enough (Trojan asteroids).' },
      { q: 'What is special about the third object in the restricted three-body problem?', opts: ['It is very light', 'It is the heaviest', 'It has the same mass as the other two'], why: 'It deals with a very light third object near two bodies orbiting each other.' },
      { q: 'In the co-rotating frame, what is conserved instead of energy?', opts: ['The Jacobi constant C', 'Specific impulse', 'The Hubble constant'], why: 'In the frame rotating with Earth and the Moon, the Jacobi constant C is conserved.' },
      { q: 'To pass through the L1 neck to the Moon, what should be done to C?', opts: ['Raise C', 'Lower C', 'C does not matter'], why: 'A large C traps the spacecraft; lowering C (adding energy) opens the L1 neck.' },
    ] },
  cw: { title: 'Relative motion and rendezvous (CW equations)', tag: 'Approaching the station ahead',
    body: `Near a station on a circular orbit a spacecraft moves in counterintuitive ways, and the CW equations describe this relative motion. They are used to plan a rendezvous, where a spacecraft approaches a station and docks. In orbit, changing your speed also changes your altitude, so relative motion defies intuition. For example, push toward the station and you rise and fall behind; drop to a lower orbit and you pull ahead. In the figure x is away from Earth (up) and y is the direction of travel (ahead); try changing the initial position and velocity.`,
    deep: String.raw`The Clohessy-Wiltshire (Hill) equations linearize the equations of motion near a reference orbit. Solution:
      <span class="tex block">x = (4-3\cos nt)x_0 + \tfrac{\sin nt}{n}\dot x_0 + \tfrac{2}{n}(1-\cos nt)\dot y_0</span>
      <span class="tex block">y = 6(\sin nt - nt)x_0 + y_0 - \tfrac{2}{n}(1-\cos nt)\dot x_0 + \tfrac{4\sin nt-3nt}{n}\dot y_0</span>
      If <span class="tex">\dot y_0 = -2nx_0</span>, you get a closed 'football orbit' that does not drift. Rendezvous with the International Space Station uses these equations to time the burns.`,
    s: ['distance above the reference orbit (away from Earth)', 'distance ahead in the direction of travel', 'angular speed of the reference orbit', 'velocity and acceleration in x'],
    r: 'The 3n²x term is the difference in gravity and centrifugal force with height; the 2nẏ and 2nẋ terms are the Coriolis effect of the rotating frame. That is why pushing forward makes you rise and pushing up makes you fall behind, the opposite of intuition.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Right behind the station, you push toward it (forward). How does the spacecraft move at first?', opts: ['Straight toward it', 'It rises and falls behind', 'It drops down', 'It stops'], why: 'More speed makes the orbit larger (higher), so it ends up circling more slowly and falls behind.' },
      { q: 'What are the CW equations mainly used to plan?', opts: ['Rendezvous and docking', 'Building launch pads', 'Measuring star distances'], why: 'They describe a spacecraft\'s motion relative to a station and are used for rendezvous planning.' },
      { q: 'In the figure\'s coordinates, which way does the y axis point?', opts: ['Away from Earth (up)', 'Direction of travel (ahead)', 'Perpendicular to the orbital plane'], why: 'x is away from Earth (up) and y is the direction of travel (ahead).' },
      { q: 'What happens to a spacecraft that drops to an orbit lower than the station?', opts: ['It falls behind', 'It pulls ahead', 'It stays in place'], why: 'Going to a lower orbit makes it pull ahead of the station.' },
    ] },
  visviva: { title: 'The vis-viva equation', tag: 'Orbital speed in one line',
    body: `The vis-viva equation gives the speed of a body at any distance along its orbit. Knowing only the orbit's size (the semi-major axis a) fixes the speed at any distance r, so it is the most used formula in orbital mechanics. For example, the circular-orbit speed, the escape speed (√2 times that), and the burns of a Hohmann transfer all come from it. Check it against a real N-body calculation in the sandbox exercise.`,
    deep: String.raw`Take energy conservation <span class="tex">\tfrac{v^2}{2} - \tfrac{\mu}{r} = \varepsilon</span> and insert the energy of an elliptical orbit, <span class="tex">\varepsilon = -\tfrac{\mu}{2a}</span>. With a → ∞ it is a parabola (escape), and with a < 0 a hyperbola; the same formula still works.`,
    s: ['speed at that point', 'gravitational parameter GM', 'distance from the center to that point', 'semi-major axis of the orbit'],
    r: 'On the same orbit, the closer (smaller r), the faster. For a circular orbit (a = r), v = √(μ/r); for an orbit that goes infinitely far (a → ∞), v = √(2μ/r), the escape speed.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'The circular-orbit speed at 1 AU is 29.8 km/s. About what is the escape speed, in km/s?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'v_esc = √2 × 29.8 ≈ 42.1 km/s.' },
      { q: 'Besides the distance r, what do you need to know to get the speed from vis-viva?', opts: ['The semi-major axis a', 'The color of the body', 'The unit of the period'], why: 'Knowing only the orbit size (semi-major axis a) fixes the speed at any distance.' },
      { q: 'On the same orbit, where is the body fastest?', opts: ['Farthest from the center', 'Closest to the center', 'The same everywhere'], why: 'On the same orbit, the smaller r, the faster.' },
      { q: 'Circular-orbit speed in low Earth orbit is 7.8 km/s. About what is the escape speed at the same height, in km/s?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'Escape speed is √2 times the circular speed, so 7.8 × 1.414 ≈ 11.0 km/s.' },
    ] },
  declination: { title: 'True north, magnetic north and declination', tag: 'A compass does not point to the pole',
    body: `North on a map (true north) and north on a compass needle (magnetic north) are different, and the angle between them is the magnetic declination <b>D</b>. True north points to the north pole of the rotation axis, while magnetic north follows the direction of Earth's magnetic field, so the two do not line up. For example, in Seoul the needle is off by about 9° to the west, so to find true north with a compass you must look 9° to the right (east) of the needle.
      What is more, the north magnetic pole moves tens of kilometers every year, from northern Canada in the 1900s toward Siberia now. So maps and navigation equipment update their declination values every few years.`,
    deep: String.raw`Earth's magnetic field is a dynamo made by moving liquid iron in the outer core; roughly, it is a bar magnet (dipole) tilted about 10° from the rotation axis plus irregular components on top. Actual declination is computed from the spherical-harmonic coefficients of the international reference field models (IGRF, WMM).
      The "dipole approximation" in the figure below is the bearing of the great circle toward the geomagnetic north pole, so it can differ from reality by a few degrees. The angle at which a compass needle dips (inclination), <span class="tex">\tan I = 2\tan\lambda_m</span>, comes from the same dipole model.`,
    s: ['magnetic declination: angle from true north to compass north (east +, west −)'],
    r: 'Add the declination to a compass bearing to get the map (true north) bearing. In Seoul D ≈ −9°, so subtract 9° from the compass bearing to get the true bearing.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'If Seoul\'s declination is −9° (west), where is true north relative to the compass needle\'s north?', opts: ['9° to the east', '9° to the west', 'The same', 'South'], why: 'The needle points 9° west of true north, so true north is 9° east (clockwise) of the needle.' },
      { q: 'What does true north point to?', opts: ['The north pole of the rotation axis', 'The direction of Earth\'s magnetic field', 'The opposite of Polaris'], why: 'True north points to the rotation axis\'s north pole; magnetic north follows the magnetic field.' },
      { q: 'Which way is the north magnetic pole moving now?', opts: ['From northern Canada toward Siberia', 'From Siberia toward Canada', 'It does not move'], why: 'The north magnetic pole moves tens of kilometers a year and is now heading toward Siberia.' },
      { q: 'In Seoul (D ≈ −9°), a compass bearing is 100°. What is the true bearing in degrees?', unit: '°', why: 'Add the declination to the compass bearing: 100° + (−9°) = 91°.' },
    ] },
  precession: { title: 'Precession and the changing pole star', tag: 'The pole star changes every 26,000 years',
    body: `Earth's rotation axis slowly traces a circle like a spinning top; this is called precession. It happens because the Moon and Sun pull on Earth's bulging equator. As a result the north celestial pole traces a circle once every 26,000 years, and the pole star changes.
      For example, when the pyramids were built (2600 BCE) the pole star was Thuban in Draco; now it is Polaris; and in about 12,000 years Vega will be near the pole. Precession is also why the stars on the Cheonsang Yeolcha Bunyajido are slightly out of place compared with today's sky.`,
    deep: String.raw`The precession period is <span class="tex">T = 2\pi/\dot\psi</span>, with general precession <span class="tex">\dot\psi = \frac{3}{2}\frac{GM}{a^3}\frac{C-A}{C\,\omega}\cos\varepsilon</span> (summing the contributions of the Moon and Sun). The vernal equinox moves west along the ecliptic by 50.3″ a year, so the right ascension and declination of stars must state their reference epoch (such as J2000).
      On top of this is a small nodding with an 18.6-year period (nutation). Hipparchus first discovered precession in the 2nd century BCE.`,
    s: ['precession rate: how far the vernal equinox moves in a year', 'precession period: the time for the axis to make one full turn'],
    r: 'The axis turns 50.29 arcseconds (about 0.014°) a year, so going all the way around 360° takes about 25,772 years. That is why the pole star changes over thousands of years.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which bright star will be near the north pole in about 12,000 years?', opts: ['Sirius', 'Vega', 'Capella', 'Arcturus'], why: 'Precession moves the north celestial pole near Vega in Lyra.' },
      { q: 'What causes precession?', opts: ['The Moon and Sun pulling on Earth\'s bulging equator', 'Changes in Earth\'s orbital speed', 'The solar wind'], why: 'The Moon and Sun pull on Earth\'s bulging equator, so the axis turns like a top.' },
      { q: 'What was the pole star when the pyramids were built (2600 BCE)?', opts: ['Polaris', 'Thuban', 'Vega'], why: 'At that time the pole star was Thuban in Draco.' },
      { q: 'About how many arcseconds does the axis turn per year?', unit: 'arcsec', why: 'The axis turns 50.29 arcseconds a year, making one turn in about 25,772 years.' },
    ] },
  paleomag: { title: 'Paleomagnetism and geomagnetic reversals', tag: 'The magnetic history written in seafloor rock',
    body: `When lava cools and hardens, the magnetite in it freezes in the direction of Earth's magnetic field at that time. So studying rocks reveals the old field direction, the paleomagnetism. This showed that Earth's magnetic field has swapped its north and south poles every few hundred thousand years; the last reversal was about 770,000 years ago (the Brunhes-Matuyama boundary).
      At mid-ocean ridges new seafloor forms and spreads to both sides, so stripes of normal and reversed polarity lie <b>symmetrically, like a mirror</b>, around the ridge. This discovery became decisive evidence for plate tectonics.`,
    deep: String.raw`A reversal takes thousands of years, and during it the field strength weakens greatly. The geomagnetic polarity time scale (GPTS) is built by matching marine magnetic anomalies, radiometric ages of volcanic rocks and sediment layers. Short reversal events (Jaramillo, Olduvai and others) are also shown in the figure below.
      Reversal intervals are irregular; in the Cretaceous there was a stretch of about 40 million years with no reversals (the Cretaceous Normal Superchron).`,
    s: ['seafloor spreading rate: how fast the plate moves away from the ridge on one side', 'duration of that magnetic polarity'],
    r: 'Rock newly hardened at the ridge remembers the field direction of the time. If the plate spreads at a steady speed, the width of the floor made during one polarity period is speed × duration. So measuring stripe widths gives the plate speed.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About how many ten-thousands of years ago was the most recent reversal (the Brunhes-Matuyama boundary)?', unit: '× 10,000 years ago', why: 'About 773,000 years ago.' },
      { q: 'Which mineral in lava remembers the old field direction?', opts: ['Magnetite', 'Quartz', 'Calcite'], why: 'When lava cools and hardens, magnetite freezes in the direction of the field at that time.' },
      { q: 'What is special about the normal and reversed stripes around a mid-ocean ridge?', opts: ['They are on one side only', 'They are mirror-symmetric', 'They are randomly placed'], why: 'New floor forms and spreads to both sides, so the stripes lie symmetrically.' },
      { q: 'If a plate spreads 2 cm per year, how wide in km is the stripe made in 500,000 years?', unit: 'km', why: 'Width is speed × time: 2 cm × 500,000 = 1,000,000 cm = 10 km.' },
    ] },
  roche: { title: 'The Roche limit', tag: 'Get too close and you break apart',
    body: `The Roche limit is the closest distance at which a moon can survive a planet's tidal force without breaking apart. When a moon gets too close, the difference in gravity between its near and far sides (the tidal force) becomes larger than the self-gravity holding it together. So inside the Roche limit material cannot gather into a single moon. For example, Saturn's rings lie mostly inside the Roche limit and never formed a moon, and Mars's moon Phobos is getting closer and is expected to break up into a ring in tens of millions of years.`,
    deep: String.raw`Setting the tidal acceleration <span class="tex">\Delta g \approx 2GMr/d^3</span> equal to the moon's surface gravity <span class="tex">Gm/r^2</span> gives the rigid-body approximation <span class="tex">d = r(2M/m)^{1/3} = 1.26R_M(\rho_M/\rho_m)^{1/3}</span>. If the moon deforms like a fluid, it stretches and breaks up farther out (2.44); real moons lie in between.
      Small objects like artificial satellites are held together by material strength, not self-gravity, so they do not break up even inside the Roche limit. In the sandbox, choosing a planet shows its Roche limit as a dotted line, and bodies that go inside it break apart.`,
    s: ['Roche limit: closer than this, a moon breaks apart', 'radius of the planet', 'mean density of the planet', 'mean density of the moon'],
    r: 'The denser the planet compared with the moon (the larger the density ratio), the farther out the limit. Multiply by 1.26 for a rigid moon and 2.44 for one that deforms like a liquid. Real moons lie in between.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What is the main reason Saturn\'s rings never formed a moon?', opts: ['They are too cold', 'They are inside the Roche limit', 'Because of the solar wind', 'They are too light'], why: 'The tidal force is greater than their self-gravity, so they cannot clump together.' },
      { q: 'What is the Roche limit?', opts: ['The closest distance at which a moon can survive tidal forces without breaking up', 'The farthest distance from which a moon escapes', 'The radius of the planet'], why: 'Inside the Roche limit the tidal force exceeds the moon\'s self-gravity.' },
      { q: 'Which moon of Mars is expected to break up into a ring?', opts: ['Deimos', 'Phobos', 'Titan'], why: 'Phobos is getting closer and is expected to break up in tens of millions of years.' },
      { q: 'How does the Roche limit of a moon that deforms like a liquid compare with a rigid one?', opts: ['Farther', 'Closer', 'The same'], why: 'A rigid moon uses 1.26 and a liquid-like one the larger 2.44, so its limit is farther.' },
    ] },
  stellar: { title: 'Stellar evolution', tag: 'Mass decides a star\'s life',
    body: `Almost everything about a star's life is decided by its mass at birth. A massive star has more fuel but burns it much faster, exploding as a supernova after only a few million years, while a light red dwarf burns for trillions of years. Below, change the mass and watch its path on the H-R diagram and its final fate.`,
    deep: String.raw`On the main sequence, hydrogen turns into helium in the core (the pp chain and the CNO cycle) while the star keeps hydrostatic equilibrium <span class="tex">dP/dr = -G M(r)\rho/r^2</span>. When the core runs out of hydrogen, it contracts and the outer layers swell into a red giant.
      Below about 8 solar masses a star leaves a planetary nebula and becomes a white dwarf (below the Chandrasekhar limit of 1.4 M☉); above that, the iron core collapses into a neutron star (below the Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff limit of about 2-3 M☉) or a black hole. The paths below are schematic.`,
    s: ['time spent on the main sequence (the hydrogen-burning period)', 'mass of the star (Sun = 1)', 'luminosity'],
    r: 'A massive star has more fuel (M times) but burns it much faster (M^3.5 times), so its lifetime is shorter as M^−2.5. A star 10 times the Sun\'s mass lives only about 30 million years.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'The main-sequence lifetime of a star 10 times heavier than the Sun is about one-what of the Sun\'s?', unit: 'th', why: 't ∝ M^−2.5, so 10^2.5 ≈ 1/316 (about 30 million years).' },
      { q: 'What decides almost everything about a star\'s life?', opts: ['Its mass at birth', 'The name of its color', 'Its distance from Earth'], why: 'Almost everything about a star\'s life is decided by its mass at birth.' },
      { q: 'How long does a light red dwarf burn?', opts: ['Millions of years', 'Trillions of years', 'Thousands of years'], why: 'A light red dwarf burns its fuel slowly, for trillions of years.' },
      { q: 'Why do massive stars have short lives?', opts: ['Fuel grows as M but the burning rate as M^3.5', 'They have little fuel', 'Their surfaces are cold'], why: 'Fuel is M times larger but it burns M^3.5 times faster, so the lifetime shortens as M^−2.5.' },
    ] },
  galaxy: { title: 'Galaxy evolution', tag: 'When the universe made the most stars',
    body: `A few hundred million years after the Big Bang the first stars and small galaxies formed, and galaxies grew by colliding and merging. In this process the universe as a whole made stars most actively about 10 billion years ago (redshift z ≈ 2, 'cosmic noon'); today the rate is about one tenth of that.
      A galaxy's appearance depends on how much gas it has left. Spiral galaxies still have gas and keep making stars, while giant elliptical galaxies have used up or lost their gas in mergers and become 'red and dead'. Our Milky Way is expected to meet the Andromeda galaxy in about 4.5 billion years and become a single elliptical galaxy.`,
    deep: String.raw`The formula above is the cosmic star formation rate density fitted by Madau and Dickinson (2014) to ultraviolet and infrared observations. Redshift is converted to time with ΛCDM cosmology (H₀ = 67.7 km/s/Mpc, Ω_m = 0.31):
      <span class="tex">t(z) = \int_z^\infty \frac{dz'}{(1+z')H(z')},\ H(z) = H_0\sqrt{\Omega_m(1+z)^3 + \Omega_\Lambda}</span>.
      Galaxy shapes are sorted by Hubble's tuning-fork scheme (elliptical, lenticular, spiral, barred spiral, irregular), and galaxies crossing the 'green valley' between the 'blue cloud' and the 'red sequence' on the color-magnitude diagram are thought to be in the middle of shutting down star formation (quenching).`,
    s: ['cosmic star formation rate density: mass of stars formed per year in 1 Mpc³', 'redshift (larger further in the past)'],
    r: 'The (1+z)^2.7 in the numerator is the part that rises into the past; the denominator is the part that falls at very early times when galaxies had not grown yet. Where the two meet, near z ≈ 2, it peaks.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About how many billion years ago was cosmic star formation most active?', unit: 'billion years ago', why: 'At redshift about 2 (cosmic age about 3.3 billion years), that is about 10 billion years ago.' },
      { q: 'When did the first stars and small galaxies form?', opts: ['A few hundred million years after the Big Bang', '10 billion years after the Big Bang', '4.5 billion years ago'], why: 'The first stars and small galaxies formed a few hundred million years after the Big Bang.' },
      { q: 'The star formation rate of the universe today is about how much of its peak?', opts: ['10 times', 'One tenth', 'The same'], why: 'Today it is about one tenth of its value at cosmic noon.' },
      { q: 'What kind of galaxy is expected when the Milky Way and Andromeda merge?', opts: ['A spiral galaxy', 'An elliptical galaxy', 'An irregular galaxy'], why: 'They are expected to meet in about 4.5 billion years and become one elliptical galaxy.' },
    ] },
  moon: { title: 'The Moon\'s formation and its retreat', tag: 'Born in a giant impact, moving away 3.8 cm a year',
    body: `How did the Moon form? The leading explanation is the <b>giant impact hypothesis</b>, in which the Moon formed when the early Earth collided with another body. About 4.5 billion years ago a Mars-sized body (Theia) struck the early Earth at a glancing angle, throwing out mantle material; this material formed a disk around Earth, and the part outside the Roche limit gathered into the Moon. The evidence is that lunar rocks have almost the same oxygen isotope ratios as Earth's mantle, and that the Moon has a small iron core.
      At its birth the Moon was much closer than now. Since then it has taken rotational energy from Earth through tidal friction and is still moving away 3.8 cm every year; in return, Earth's day is getting longer. You can watch the impact in the sandbox scenario "Birth of the Moon".`,
    deep: String.raw`Because of Earth's rotation the tidal bulge runs ahead of the Moon and pulls it forward (transferring angular momentum), while Earth's rotation slows. Running today's rate backward would put the Moon touching Earth about 1.5 billion years ago (the gray curve below), which does not match the geological record.
      In the past the shape of the oceans was different and tidal resonance was weaker, so the Moon receded more slowly. Tidal deposits of the Elatina Formation 620 million years ago show a day of about 21.9 hours and an Earth-Moon distance of about 58 Earth radii (the dot below).`,
    s: ['Earth-Moon distance', 'rate at which the distance increases'],
    r: 'The closer the Moon, the stronger the tides and the faster it recedes (as the −11/2 power of distance). So it seems it should have receded faster in the past, but in fact the different shape of the oceans made long periods slower.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'How many cm per year is the Moon now moving away from Earth?', unit: 'cm', why: 'Lunar laser ranging gives about 3.82 cm per year.' },
      { q: 'What is the leading explanation for the Moon\'s origin?', opts: ['The giant impact hypothesis', 'Capture by Earth', 'Splitting off from the Sun'], why: 'It is the giant impact hypothesis: a Mars-sized body, Theia, hit the early Earth and the Moon formed.' },
      { q: 'Which is NOT evidence for the giant impact hypothesis?', opts: ['Lunar rocks and Earth\'s mantle have almost the same oxygen isotope ratios', 'The Moon has a small iron core', 'The Moon has a thick atmosphere'], why: 'The oxygen isotope ratios and the small iron core are the evidence.' },
      { q: 'What happens to Earth as the price of the Moon moving away?', opts: ['The day gets longer', 'The day gets shorter', 'The orbital period gets shorter'], why: 'The Moon recedes by taking Earth\'s rotational energy through tidal friction, so Earth\'s day gets longer.' },
    ] },
  coords: { title: 'The celestial sphere and coordinates', tag: 'How to write an address in the sky',
    body: `If you think of the sky as a big ball around Earth (the celestial sphere), you can write star positions as coordinates. Then a star's position can be written as <b>declination δ</b> and <b>right ascension α</b>, like latitude and longitude on Earth. On the other hand, how high a star looks in my sky (altitude h) is set by the observer's latitude φ and the star's hour angle H, so the same star appears at different heights depending on place and time.
      For example, the higher the latitude, the more stars never set (circumpolar stars), and stars with declination below −(90° − φ) never rise at all. Also, the north celestial pole (near Polaris) always stands at a height equal to the observer's latitude, so in Seoul (37.5°N) it is about 37.5° above the horizon.`,
    deep: String.raw`Applying the spherical law of cosines to the astronomical triangle (zenith, north celestial pole, star) gives the formula above. The azimuth is <span class="tex">\tan A = \frac{\sin H}{\cos H\sin\varphi - \tan\delta\cos\varphi}</span>. The hour angle is local sidereal time minus right ascension, <span class="tex">H = \text{LST} - \alpha</span>.`,
    s: ['altitude: angle measured up from the horizon', 'latitude of the observer', 'declination: angle north of the celestial equator (like latitude on Earth)', 'hour angle: time since the star crossed the meridian, as an angle (1 hour = 15°)'],
    r: 'How high a star appears is set by three things: where I am (latitude), where the star sits on the celestial sphere (declination), and how long it has been since the star passed its highest point in the southern sky (hour angle). At H = 0 the star is highest (on the meridian), with altitude 90° − |φ − δ|.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'In Seoul, at latitude 37.5°, how many degrees above the horizon is the north celestial pole (near Polaris)?', unit: '°', why: 'The altitude of the north celestial pole equals the observer\'s latitude.' },
      { q: 'Which two coordinates give a star\'s position on the celestial sphere?', opts: ['Declination and right ascension', 'Altitude and azimuth', 'Latitude and longitude'], why: 'A star\'s position on the celestial sphere is written as declination δ and right ascension α.' },
      { q: 'As latitude increases, what happens to the number of stars that never set (circumpolar stars)?', opts: ['It increases', 'It decreases', 'It does not change'], why: 'The higher the latitude, the more circumpolar stars.' },
      { q: 'At latitude 37.5°, what is the altitude in degrees of a star with declination 20° when it crosses the meridian?', unit: '°', why: 'The meridian altitude is 90° − |37.5° − 20°| = 72.5°.' },
    ] },
  eot: { title: 'The equation of time and the analemma', tag: 'Why a sundial disagrees with a clock',
    body: `A sundial and a wristwatch do not agree all year round, and the difference is called the equation of time. A clock runs at the same rate all year, but the real Sun crosses the meridian up to ±16 minutes early or late. This is because Earth's orbit is an ellipse, so its orbital speed changes (Kepler's second law), and because its axis is tilted. So photographing the Sun at the same clock time throughout the year gives a figure-eight called the <b>analemma</b>.`,
    deep: String.raw`The equation of time is the sum of two parts: a yearly term from eccentricity (amplitude about 7.7 minutes) and a half-yearly term from the obliquity (amplitude about 9.9 minutes). Precise values are computed from the difference between the Sun's apparent right ascension and that of the mean Sun. On Mars the eccentricity is larger, so the analemma is teardrop-shaped.`,
    s: ['equation of time: sundial time minus clock time (minutes)', 'day number counted from January 1 (1-365)', 'the date converted to an angle around one year'],
    r: 'A clock always runs at the same rate, but the Sun\'s meridian crossing runs up to 16 minutes early or late over the year. The sine 2B term is the half-yearly difference from the tilted axis; the cosine B and sine B terms are the yearly difference from the elliptical orbit.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About how many minutes is the largest equation of time in a year?', unit: 'min', why: 'About +16.4 minutes in early November and about −14.2 minutes in mid-February.' },
      { q: 'If you photograph the Sun at the same time every day for a year, what shape appears?', opts: ['A circle', 'A figure-eight (the analemma)', 'A straight line'], why: 'The equation of time combined with the Sun\'s changing declination gives the figure-eight analemma.' },
      { q: 'What are the two causes of the equation of time?', opts: ['The elliptical orbit and the axial tilt', 'The Moon\'s pull and the solar wind', 'Atmospheric refraction and clouds'], why: 'The orbit is elliptical so orbital speed changes, and the axis is tilted.' },
      { q: 'What causes the sine 2B (half-yearly) term in the equation of time?', opts: ['The axial tilt', 'The elliptical shape of the orbit', 'Precession'], why: 'The sine 2B term comes from the axial tilt; the cosine B and sine B terms from the elliptical orbit.' },
    ] },
  seasons: { title: 'Seasons and the tilt of the axis', tag: 'Summer is not hot because we are closer',
    body: `Why are there summers and winters? Many people think it is because of the distance to the Sun, but Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January. The Northern Hemisphere still has winter then because the axis is tilted 23.4°, so sunlight comes in at a low angle and the days are short. Furthermore, when this tilt and the eccentricity change over cycles of tens of thousands of years, ice ages arrive; these are the <b>Milankovitch cycles</b>. Below, change the tilt to see how the daily average sunlight at each latitude changes.`,
    deep: String.raw`h₀ is the sunrise hour angle, <span class="tex">\cos h_0 = -\tan\varphi\tan\delta</span>, and S₀ = 1361 W/m² (the solar constant). The axial tilt varies between 22.1° and 24.5° over about 41,000 years, the eccentricity between 0 and 0.06 over about 100,000 and 400,000 years, and the season of perihelion over about 23,000 years (precession).`,
    s: ['daily average sunlight energy received by 1 m² of ground (W/m²)', 'solar constant: intensity of sunlight at Earth\'s distance, 1,361 W/m²', 'ratio of mean distance to current distance (larger when closer)', 'sunrise hour angle: sets the length of the day', 'latitude and the Sun\'s declination'],
    r: 'Sunlight received by the ground depends on how high the Sun rises (the sine terms) and how long the day is (h₀). In summer the Sun\'s declination δ grows, so both increase. The distance term (a/r)² changes by only about 7%, so it is not the main cause of the seasons.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'When is Earth closest to the Sun (perihelion)?', opts: ['Early January', 'Early April', 'Early July', 'Early October'], why: 'Perihelion is around January 3-4. Seasons are caused by the axial tilt, not the distance.' },
      { q: 'How many degrees is Earth\'s axis tilted?', unit: '°', why: 'The axis is tilted 23.4°, which causes the seasons.' },
      { q: 'What is the main reason for winter in the Northern Hemisphere?', opts: ['It is far from the Sun', 'Sunlight comes in at a low angle and days are short', 'There are many clouds'], why: 'Because of the tilt, not the distance, sunlight is slanted and the days are short.' },
      { q: 'What do we call the changes in tilt and eccentricity over tens of thousands of years that bring ice ages?', opts: ['Milankovitch cycles', 'The sunspot cycle', 'The saros cycle'], why: 'These long-term changes are the Milankovitch cycles.' },
    ] },
  moonphase: { title: 'Phases of the Moon and eclipses', tag: 'Why not every full moon brings an eclipse',
    body: `Over a month the Moon waxes and wanes, changing its shape (phase). The Moon does not shine by itself but reflects sunlight, so its phase changes with the angle between the Sun, Earth and Moon. This arrangement is also linked to eclipses: at new moon, when the Sun and Moon overlap, there is a solar eclipse; at full moon, when the Moon enters Earth's shadow, there is a lunar eclipse. But because the Moon's orbit is tilted 5.1° to the ecliptic, eclipses do not happen every month, only when the Moon is near a node.`,
    deep: String.raw`The times when eclipses are possible (eclipse seasons) come twice a year, about 173 days apart. Because the nodes turn with an 18.6-year period, eclipses with the same conditions repeat every saros (6,585.3 days ≈ 18 years 11 days). The illuminated fraction k is set by the elongation ψ (the Sun-Moon angle).`,
    s: ['fraction of the Moon that appears lit (0 = new, 1 = full)', 'phase angle: angle between the Sun and Earth as seen from the Moon'],
    r: 'It tells how much of the sunlit half of the Moon faces Earth. At ψ = 0° (Earth directly facing the lit side) k = 1, a full moon; at ψ = 180°, k = 0, a new moon.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'At which phase of the Moon can a lunar eclipse happen?', opts: ['New moon', 'First quarter', 'Full moon', 'Last quarter'], why: 'Earth must be between the Sun and Moon, so it happens only at full moon.' },
      { q: 'Why does the Moon\'s phase change?', opts: ['The Moon changes its own brightness', 'The Sun-Earth-Moon angle changes', 'Earth\'s shadow covers it every day'], why: 'The Moon reflects sunlight, so its phase changes with the Sun-Earth-Moon angle.' },
      { q: 'At which phase of the Moon can a solar eclipse happen?', opts: ['New moon', 'Full moon', 'First quarter'], why: 'A solar eclipse happens when the Sun and Moon overlap at new moon.' },
      { q: 'Why do eclipses not happen every month?', opts: ['The Moon\'s orbit is tilted 5.1° to the ecliptic', 'The Moon is too small', 'Because of Earth\'s rotation'], why: 'Eclipses happen only when the Moon is near a node.' },
    ] },
  tides: { title: 'Tides', tag: 'Why the sea bulges twice a day',
    body: `At the seashore the water comes in and goes out twice a day; these are the tides. The Moon pulls the near side of Earth harder and the far side less, and that difference is the tidal force. Because of it the ocean bulges in two places, toward the Moon and on the opposite side, so high tide comes twice a day. The Sun also exerts a tidal force, but only about 46% as strong as the Moon's; when the two are in line the forces add to give spring tides (large tides), and when they are at right angles they partly cancel to give neap tides (small tides).`,
    deep: String.raw`The tidal force is inversely proportional to the cube of distance, so although the Sun is 27 million times heavier than the Moon, it is 390 times farther away, and its tidal effect is <span class="tex">(M_\odot/M_☾)(d_☾/d_\odot)^3 \approx 0.46</span>. The actual tidal range varies greatly with coastal shape and ocean resonance (about 9 m at Incheon on the Yellow Sea). Tidal friction slows Earth's rotation and pushes the Moon away.`,
    s: ['tidal acceleration: difference in pull between Earth\'s near side and its center', 'gravitational constant', 'mass of the pulling body (Moon, Sun)', 'Earth\'s radius', 'distance to that body'],
    r: 'Tides come not from the pull itself but from the "difference" in pull. The difference is inversely proportional to the cube of distance, so the nearby Moon makes tides more than twice as large as the much heavier Sun.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'The Sun\'s tidal force is about what percentage of the Moon\'s?', unit: '%', why: 'The Sun is much heavier, but tidal force falls with the cube of distance, so it is about 46% of the Moon\'s.' },
      { q: 'How many high tides usually come in a day?', unit: 'times', why: 'The ocean bulges toward the Moon and on the opposite side, so high tide comes twice a day.' },
      { q: 'What is the large tide when the Sun and Moon are in line called?', opts: ['Spring tide', 'Neap tide', 'Slack water'], why: 'In line, the forces add to give a spring tide; at right angles, a neap tide.' },
      { q: 'How does tidal force change with distance?', opts: ['Inversely with distance', 'Inversely with the square of distance', 'Inversely with the cube of distance'], why: 'The difference in pull falls with the cube of distance, so the nearby Moon makes larger tides.' },
    ] },
  teq: { title: 'Equilibrium temperature and the greenhouse effect', tag: 'Why Venus is hotter than Mercury',
    body: `The equilibrium temperature is the temperature at which the sunlight a planet absorbs equals the infrared it gives off. It is computed without the atmosphere, so comparing it with the real temperature shows what the atmosphere does. For example, Earth's equilibrium temperature is −18 °C, but its actual average is 15 °C. That is because greenhouse gases in the atmosphere trap infrared and warm the surface; this is the greenhouse effect. As an extreme case, Venus, with its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere, has a surface of 460 °C, hotter than Mercury, which is closer to the Sun.`,
    deep: String.raw`It comes from absorption <span class="tex">\pi R^2(1-A)S</span> = emission <span class="tex">4\pi R^2\sigma T^4</span>. In a one-layer atmosphere model with infrared absorptivity ε, <span class="tex">T_s = T_\text{eq}\left(\frac{2}{2-\varepsilon}\right)^{1/4}</span>. For a thick atmosphere like Venus's, a multi-layer model (optical depth τ) gives <span class="tex">T_s^4 \approx T_\text{eq}^4(1 + \tfrac34\tau)</span>.`,
    s: ['equilibrium temperature: where absorbed sunlight equals emitted heat', 'reflectivity (albedo): the fraction of sunlight bounced back', 'brightness of the star (Sun = 1)', 'distance to the star (AU)'],
    r: 'The closer the planet and the less it reflects, the hotter it is. Four times the distance gives half the temperature (the −1/2 power). The real surface temperature adds the greenhouse effect on top.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'With albedo 0.3, about what is Earth\'s equilibrium temperature in °C?', unit: '°C', why: 'About 255 K = −18 °C. The difference from the real average of 15 °C is the greenhouse effect.' },
      { q: 'About what is Earth\'s actual average temperature?', opts: ['−18 °C', '15 °C', '460 °C'], why: 'The equilibrium temperature is −18 °C, but the greenhouse effect makes the real average 15 °C.' },
      { q: 'Why is Venus hotter than Mercury?', opts: ['The greenhouse effect of its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere', 'It is closer to the Sun', 'It spins fast'], why: 'Its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere makes the surface 460 °C.' },
      { q: 'A planet 4 times farther from the Sun has what multiple of the equilibrium temperature?', unit: '×', why: 'Temperature goes as distance to the −1/2 power, so four times the distance gives half.' },
    ] },
  hz: { title: 'The habitable zone', tag: 'The distance where water can be liquid',
    body: `The habitable zone is the range of distances around a star where liquid water can exist on a planet's surface. Too close to the star and the oceans boil away; too far and they freeze, so only in between can we expect life. This distance depends on the star's brightness: the brighter the star, the farther out it lies, and for dim red dwarfs it is very close. So planets around red dwarfs, being close to the star, easily become tidally locked, with only one side facing the star.`,
    deep: String.raw`The inner edge is set by the runaway greenhouse effect and the outer edge by the maximum greenhouse limit (carbon dioxide condensation) (Kasting 1993, Kopparapu 2013). As a star ages it brightens and the habitable zone moves outward. In about a billion years the Sun will be bright enough to push Earth past the inner edge.`,
    s: ['inner and outer edges of the habitable zone (AU)', 'brightness of the star (Sun = 1)'],
    r: 'Light received is inversely proportional to the square of distance, so for a star 4 times brighter, the distance with the same temperature is √4 = 2 times farther.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'For a star 4 times as bright as the Sun, the habitable zone is how many times farther than the Sun\'s?', unit: '×', why: 'Distance goes as √L, so √4 = 2 times.' },
      { q: 'The habitable zone is the range of distances where what can exist?', opts: ['Liquid water', 'Only ice', 'Hydrogen gas'], why: 'It is the range of distances where liquid water can exist on a planet\'s surface.' },
      { q: 'Where is the habitable zone of a dim red dwarf?', opts: ['Very close', 'Very far', 'At the same distance as the Sun\'s'], why: 'The dimmer the star, the closer the habitable zone.' },
      { q: 'For a star 9 times as bright as the Sun, the habitable zone is how many times farther than the Sun\'s?', unit: '×', why: 'Distance goes as the square root of brightness, so √9 = 3 times.' },
    ] },
  blackbody: { title: 'Blackbody radiation: star color and temperature', tag: 'Red stars are cool, blue stars are hot',
    body: `A hot object gives off light with a shape set by its temperature; this is called blackbody radiation. Starlight is nearly blackbody radiation, so a star's color reveals its surface temperature. The higher the temperature, the brighter it gets, and the wavelength of the strongest light gets shorter, so it looks bluer (Wien's law). Specifically, the wavelength of the strongest light is 2,898 μm·K divided by the temperature, so the Sun (about 5,800 K) is brightest at about 500 nm, blue-green light.`,
    deep: String.raw`Integrating the Planck function <span class="tex">B_\lambda(T) = \frac{2hc^2}{\lambda^5}\frac{1}{e^{hc/\lambda k T}-1}</span> over all wavelengths gives the Stefan-Boltzmann law <span class="tex">\sigma T^4</span>, and setting its derivative to zero gives Wien's displacement law. A star's spectrum also carries dark lines (absorption lines) from elements in its atmosphere, which reveal its composition.`,
    s: ['wavelength of the strongest light', 'surface temperature (K)', 'total energy a star emits per second (luminosity)', 'radius of the star', 'Stefan-Boltzmann constant'],
    r: 'Double the temperature and the wavelength of the strongest light halves (bluer), while at the same size the brightness becomes 2⁴ = 16 times. Double the size and the brightness becomes 4 times.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'For a star with surface temperature 5,800 K, about what is the wavelength of the strongest light, in nm?', unit: 'nm', why: '2898 μm·K / 5800 K ≈ 0.50 μm = 500 nm.' },
      { q: 'What color does a hot star look?', opts: ['Blue', 'Red', 'It does not depend on temperature'], why: 'The hotter the star, the shorter the wavelength of the strongest light, so it looks blue.' },
      { q: 'If a star of the same size doubles its temperature, how many times brighter does it get?', unit: '×', why: 'Brightness goes as the fourth power of temperature, so 2⁴ = 16 times.' },
      { q: 'At the same temperature, if a star\'s radius doubles, its brightness becomes', opts: ['2 times', '4 times', '16 times'], why: 'Twice the size means 4 times the surface area, so 4 times the brightness.' },
    ] },
  magnitude: { title: 'Star brightness, magnitude and distance', tag: 'Five magnitudes is a factor of 100',
    body: `Star brightness is given in magnitudes: the smaller the number, the brighter, and a difference of 5 magnitudes is exactly a factor of 100. The brightness seen from Earth is the apparent magnitude m, and the brightness the star would have at 10 parsecs (32.6 light-years) is the absolute magnitude M. Apparent brightness depends on distance, so knowing the difference between the two (the distance modulus) tells you the distance to the star.`,
    deep: String.raw`The magnitude system is Hipparchus's six classes, set by Pogson (1856) so that <span class="tex">2.512^5 = 100</span>. Adding extinction A by interstellar dust gives <span class="tex">m - M = 5\log_{10} d - 5 + A</span>. 'Standard candles' of known absolute magnitude, such as Cepheid variables, measure distances to galaxies.`,
    s: ['apparent magnitude: brightness seen from Earth', 'absolute magnitude: brightness if placed at 10 pc', 'distance (parsecs)', 'brightness ratio of two stars'],
    r: 'The larger the magnitude number, the fainter. Each time m − M (the distance modulus) grows by 5, the distance is 10 times greater. A difference of 1 magnitude is a factor of 2.512 in brightness.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'A difference of 5 magnitudes is a brightness factor of how much?', unit: '×', why: 'By Pogson\'s definition it is exactly 100 times.' },
      { q: 'The smaller the magnitude number, the star is', opts: ['brighter', 'fainter', 'farther'], why: 'The smaller the magnitude number, the brighter.' },
      { q: 'Absolute magnitude is the brightness of a star placed at how many parsecs?', unit: 'pc', why: 'Absolute magnitude is the brightness the star would have at 10 parsecs (32.6 light-years).' },
      { q: 'If the distance modulus m − M grows by 5, the distance becomes', opts: ['5 times', '10 times', '100 times'], why: 'Each time m − M grows by 5, the distance is 10 times greater.' },
    ] },
  remnants: { title: 'The end of stars: white dwarfs, neutron stars, black holes', tag: 'What holds back the collapse',
    body: `What is left when a star uses up its fuel? When the fuel runs out, the pressure that held off gravity disappears and the star contracts, and its final form depends on what stops that contraction. If the force that keeps electrons from entering the same state (electron degeneracy pressure) holds, it becomes an Earth-sized white dwarf; if that is exceeded, it becomes a neutron star 20-25 km across, held up by neutron degeneracy pressure. Beyond that, nothing can hold and it becomes a black hole. The radius of a black hole's event horizon is directly proportional to its mass: about 3 km for one solar mass, and about 30 km for ten.`,
    deep: String.raw`Chandrasekhar limit about 1.4 M☉, TOV limit about 2.2-2.9 M☉. Near a black hole time runs slowly (gravitational time dilation). The innermost stable circular orbit (ISCO) is at <span class="tex">3r_s</span>, and the photon sphere where light travels in a circle is at <span class="tex">1.5r_s</span>. In the sandbox, replacing the Sun with a black hole of the same mass leaves the planets' orbits unchanged, because gravity seen from outside depends only on mass.`,
    s: ['Schwarzschild radius (event horizon)', 'gravitational constant', 'mass', 'speed of light', 'how many times faster time runs far away than near the black hole', 'distance from the center'],
    r: 'The size of the horizon is directly proportional to mass. As you approach the horizon (r → r_s) the denominator approaches 0, so time there appears almost stopped when seen from far away.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About how many km is the event horizon radius of a one-solar-mass black hole?', unit: 'km', why: 'r_s = 2GM/c² ≈ 2.95 km.' },
      { q: 'What is the end state of a star held up by electron degeneracy pressure?', opts: ['A white dwarf', 'A neutron star', 'A black hole'], why: 'If electron degeneracy pressure holds, it becomes an Earth-sized white dwarf.' },
      { q: 'About how large is a neutron star across?', opts: ['The size of Earth', '20-25 km', 'The size of the Sun'], why: 'A neutron star held up by neutron degeneracy pressure is 20-25 km across.' },
      { q: 'About how many km is the event horizon radius of a black hole of 10 solar masses?', unit: 'km', why: 'The horizon radius is directly proportional to mass, so 3 km × 10 = 30 km.' },
    ] },
  hubble: { title: 'Hubble\'s law and cosmic expansion', tag: 'The farther the galaxy, the faster it recedes',
    body: `In 1929 Hubble compared the distances of galaxies with the redshifts of their spectra and showed that the farther a galaxy is, the faster it moves away. This is Hubble's law. It means that galaxies are not running away through space; space itself is stretching. The slope H₀ linking distance and recession speed is the Hubble constant, and the recession speed is H₀ times the distance. The inverse of H₀ also gives a rough value of the age of the universe.`,
    deep: String.raw`At small distances <span class="tex">z \approx v/c</span>. Measurements of H₀ split into about 67.4 (cosmic microwave background, Planck) and 73 (Cepheids and supernovae, SH0ES) km/s/Mpc depending on method, which is called the 'Hubble tension'. The actual age of the universe, 13.8 billion years, is found by integrating the changing expansion rate (deceleration then acceleration).`,
    s: ['speed at which the galaxy recedes', 'Hubble constant (km/s/Mpc)', 'distance to the galaxy (Mpc)', 'Hubble time: the time to run the expansion back to a single point'],
    r: 'A galaxy twice as far recedes twice as fast. With H₀ = 70, every 1 Mpc (3.26 million light-years) farther adds 70 km/s. 1/H₀ is about 14 billion years, close to the age of the universe.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'With H₀ = 70 km/s/Mpc, what is the recession speed in km/s of a galaxy 200 Mpc away?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'v = 70 × 200 = 14,000 km/s.' },
      { q: 'According to Hubble\'s law, the farther a galaxy is, the more it', opts: ['recedes quickly', 'approaches quickly', 'stays still'], why: 'The greater the distance, the greater the recession speed.' },
      { q: 'What does Hubble\'s law mean?', opts: ['Galaxies are running away through space', 'Space itself is stretching', 'Our galaxy is the center of the universe'], why: 'It means not that galaxies are running away, but that space itself is stretching.' },
      { q: 'The inverse of H₀ (1/H₀) is about how many hundred million years?', unit: '× 100 million years', why: '1/H₀ is about 14 billion years, close to the age of the universe.' },
    ] },
  cmb: { title: 'The cosmic microwave background', tag: 'A fossil of light from 13.8 billion years ago',
    body: `Whichever way you look in the sky, faint microwaves arrive; this is the cosmic microwave background. At first the universe was hot and dense, and light was trapped by matter. Then about 380,000 years later, as it cooled to 3,000 K and atoms formed, light could travel freely. The wavelength of that light has since been stretched 1,100 times by cosmic expansion, so it is now observed as 2.7 K microwaves. This light was discovered by accident in 1965 by Penzias and Wilson.`,
    deep: String.raw`The cosmic microwave background is the most perfect blackbody spectrum ever measured (COBE/FIRAS). Temperature fluctuations at the level of one part in 100,000 (WMAP, Planck) are the density seeds that later became galaxies, and their angular size tells us that the universe is flat and gives the proportions of dark matter and dark energy.`,
    s: ['temperature of the background radiation at redshift z', 'redshift: how much the universe stretched while the light traveled, minus 1'],
    r: 'If the universe stretches 2 times, the wavelength of light also doubles and the temperature halves. When the light now at 2.7 K was released, the universe was 1,100 times smaller and about 3,000 K.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About what was the temperature in K when the cosmic microwave background was released (z ≈ 1,100)?', unit: 'K', why: '2.7255 × 1,101 ≈ 3,000 K.' },
      { q: 'As what kind of light is the cosmic microwave background observed today?', opts: ['Microwaves', 'Gamma rays', 'Visible light'], why: 'Its wavelength has stretched, so it is now observed as 2.7 K microwaves.' },
      { q: 'About how many ten-thousands of years after the Big Bang could light travel freely?', unit: '× 10,000 years', why: 'About 380,000 years later, atoms formed and light became free.' },
      { q: 'If the universe stretches 2 times, the background radiation temperature becomes', opts: ['2 times', 'half', 'unchanged'], why: 'The wavelength doubles, so the temperature halves.' },
    ] },
  gravity: { title: 'Universal gravitation', tag: 'The apple and the Moon under one law',
    body: `Everything with mass attracts everything else, and this force is called universal gravitation. It is proportional to the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance. Newton showed that the force that makes an apple fall is the same one that holds the Moon, explaining motion on Earth and in the sky with one law. This law also explains why surface gravity differs from planet to planet: surface gravity is proportional to the planet's mass and inversely proportional to the square of its radius.`,
    deep: String.raw`G = 6.674×10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² was first measured by Cavendish in 1798 with a torsion balance. Outside a spherically symmetric body, you can calculate as if all its mass were at the center (the shell theorem). Going inside Earth, the pull of the outer shell cancels out, so gravity decreases.`,
    s: ['force with which two bodies attract each other', 'gravitational constant 6.674×10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²', 'masses of the two bodies', 'distance between the two centers', 'gravitational acceleration: how fast objects fall at the surface'],
    r: 'Double the mass and the force doubles; double the distance and the force is a quarter. A planet\'s surface g is proportional to its mass and inversely proportional to the square of its radius.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'A planet with the same radius as Earth but twice the mass has how many times Earth\'s surface gravity?', unit: '×', why: 'g = GM/r²; with the same radius it is proportional to mass.' },
      { q: 'If the distance between two bodies doubles, gravity becomes', opts: ['half', 'a quarter', 'twice'], why: 'The force is inversely proportional to the square of distance, so a quarter.' },
      { q: 'What did Newton show?', opts: ['The force that drops an apple is the one that holds the Moon', 'The Moon feels no gravity', 'Heavy objects fall faster'], why: 'Newton explained motion on Earth and in the sky with one law.' },
      { q: 'A planet with the same mass but twice the radius has how many times the surface gravity?', unit: '×', why: 'Surface gravity is inversely proportional to the square of the radius, so 1/4 = 0.25 times.' },
    ] },
  keplerlaws: { title: 'Kepler\'s three laws', tag: 'Ellipses, equal areas, period and size',
    body: `Kepler analyzed records of planetary observations and summed up planetary motion in three laws. First, a planet moves on an ellipse with the Sun at one focus. Second, the line joining the Sun and planet sweeps out equal areas in equal times, so the planet moves faster when it is near the Sun. Third, the square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis. In particular, around the Sun, with the period in years and the semi-major axis in AU, T² = a³ holds exactly. The shaded sectors below are all areas swept out in the same amount of time.`,
    deep: String.raw`Kepler found these laws empirically from Tycho Brahe's observations of Mars (1609, 1619), and Newton derived them from universal gravitation. The second law follows from conservation of angular momentum, <span class="tex">\tfrac12 r^2\dot\theta = h/2</span>, and the third is quickly checked for a circular orbit with <span class="tex">GM/r^2 = (2\pi/T)^2 r</span>.`,
    s: ['orbital period', 'semi-major axis (the average size of the orbit)', 'gravitational constant', 'mass of the central body'],
    r: 'T² divided by a³ is the same for everything orbiting the same body. It is set only by the mass of the central body, so measuring a moon\'s period and distance gives the planet\'s mass.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What is the orbital period in years of an orbit with a semi-major axis of 4 AU?', unit: 'years', why: 'T² = a³, so T = √64 = 8 years.' },
      { q: 'According to Kepler\'s first law, a planet\'s orbit is', opts: ['an ellipse with the Sun at one focus', 'a circle centered on the Sun', 'a straight line'], why: 'A planet moves on an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.' },
      { q: 'According to Kepler\'s second law, when a planet is close to the Sun it', opts: ['moves fast', 'moves slowly', 'stops'], why: 'Equal areas are swept in equal times, so it is faster when close.' },
      { q: 'What can you learn by measuring a moon\'s period and distance?', opts: ['The mass of the central planet', 'The moon\'s color', 'The moon\'s temperature'], why: 'T²/a³ is set only by the mass of the central body.' },
    ] },
  parallax: { title: 'Measuring star distances with parallax', tag: 'One arcsecond, one parsec',
    body: `As Earth goes around the Sun, nearby stars shift back and forth a little against the distant background; half of that angle is the annual parallax p. The nearer the star, the larger the shift, so measuring this angle gives the distance to the star directly. Distance is inversely proportional to parallax: if p is 1 arcsecond (1/3600°), the distance is 1 parsec (3.26 light-years). But this angle is very small; even the nearest star, Proxima, shows only 0.77 arcseconds, and a stellar parallax was first measured only in 1838.`,
    deep: String.raw`From the small-angle approximation <span class="tex">\tan p \approx p = 1\,\text{AU}/d</span>, 1 pc = 206,265 AU. The Hipparcos satellite (1989-1993) measured parallaxes to about 1 milliarcsecond, and Gaia measured 1.8 billion stars to tens of microarcseconds. This is the first rung of the cosmic distance ladder.`,
    s: ['distance to the star (parsecs)', 'annual parallax (arcseconds)'],
    r: 'The smaller the parallax, the farther the star. One parsec is the distance at which the parallax is 1 arcsecond, 3.26 light-years. A parallax of 0.1″ means 10 pc.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'How many parsecs away is a star with a parallax of 0.1 arcsecond?', unit: 'pc', why: 'd = 1/p = 10 pc.' },
      { q: 'A star with a small parallax is', opts: ['closer', 'farther', 'unrelated to distance'], why: 'Distance is inversely proportional to parallax, so the smaller the parallax, the farther.' },
      { q: 'About how many light-years is 1 parsec?', unit: 'light-years', why: 'One parsec is the distance with a parallax of 1 arcsecond, 3.26 light-years.' },
      { q: 'In what year was a stellar parallax first measured?', opts: ['1609', '1838', '1929'], why: 'The angle is so small that it was first measured only in 1838.' },
    ] },
  doppler: { title: 'Spectra and the Doppler effect', tag: 'Measuring speed from the color of light',
    body: `Just as an ambulance siren sounds different coming toward you and going away, the wavelength of starlight changes with the star's motion. This is the Doppler effect. When starlight is spread out with a prism, dark absorption lines appear at wavelengths fixed for each element, and the positions of these lines are the reference. If the star moves away from us, the lines shift to longer wavelengths (toward red); if it approaches, to shorter wavelengths (toward blue). So measuring this shift gives the speed along the line of sight. In practice, the speeds of binary stars, exoplanet hosts and receding galaxies are all measured this way.`,
    deep: String.raw`Near the speed of light, the relativistic formula <span class="tex">1+z = \sqrt{\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}}</span> is used. The redshift of distant galaxies is not from galaxies racing through space but from space stretching while the light traveled (cosmological redshift), so <span class="tex">1+z = a_\text{now}/a_\text{then}</span>. Today's spectrographs can measure a star's wobble of tens of centimeters per second.`,
    s: ['amount the wavelength is shifted', 'wavelength at rest', 'line-of-sight speed (+ when receding)', 'speed of light'],
    r: 'The wavelength stretches or shrinks by the ratio of the speed to the speed of light. Receding at 300 km/s makes the wavelength 0.1% longer.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'The 656.3 nm Hα line appears at 657.0 nm. The star is', opts: ['approaching', 'receding', 'at rest'], why: 'It shifted to longer wavelength (redshift), so it is receding, at about 320 km/s.' },
      { q: 'If a star approaches us, which way do its spectral lines shift?', opts: ['Toward blue (shorter wavelength)', 'Toward red (longer wavelength)', 'They do not move'], why: 'Approaching shifts them to shorter wavelengths; receding, to longer.' },
      { q: 'Which speed can the Doppler effect reveal?', opts: ['Speed along the line of sight', 'Speed across the sky', 'Rotation period'], why: 'Measuring the line shift gives the speed along the line of sight.' },
      { q: 'If the wavelength is 0.2% longer, how many km/s is the star receding?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'The fractional change is v/c, so 0.002 × 300,000 km/s = 600 km/s.' },
    ] },
  transit: { title: 'Finding exoplanets: the transit method', tag: 'When starlight dims just a little',
    body: `When a planet passes in front of its star, the starlight dims slightly, and the transit method uses this to find exoplanets. When the planet covers the star, the light drops by the planet's cross-section, which is proportional to the square of its radius. For example, Jupiter passing in front of the Sun dims it by 1%, and Earth by only 0.008%. Tiny as these changes are, the Kepler and TESS space telescopes caught them and found thousands of exoplanets. The spacing between dips gives the orbital period, and the depth gives the planet's size.`,
    deep: String.raw`Transit duration <span class="tex">t \approx \frac{P}{\pi}\frac{R_\star}{a}</span> (for a central crossing); the chance that the orbit is aligned is about <span class="tex">R_\star/a</span> (0.5% for an Earth-like planet). Used together with the radial velocity method, both mass and radius are known, giving the density (rocky or gaseous). During a transit, starlight passing through the planet's atmosphere picks up absorption that reveals its composition (JWST).`,
    s: ['fractional dimming', 'radius of the planet', 'radius of the star'],
    r: 'It is the area the planet covers, πR_p², divided by the star\'s area, πR_⋆². So measuring the dimming gives the planet\'s size relative to the star.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'A Jupiter-sized planet (1/10 of the Sun\'s radius) crosses a Sun-like star. By what percentage does it dim?', unit: '%', why: '(1/10)² = 1%.' },
      { q: 'In the transit method, what does the spacing between dips tell you?', opts: ['The orbital period', 'The planet\'s temperature', 'The planet\'s mass'], why: 'The spacing gives the orbital period, and the depth gives the planet\'s size.' },
      { q: 'Which space telescopes found thousands of exoplanets with the transit method?', opts: ['Kepler and TESS', 'Hubble and Chandra', 'Voyager and Pioneer'], why: 'Kepler and TESS caught the tiny brightness changes.' },
      { q: 'If the planet\'s radius doubles, the dimming becomes', opts: ['2 times', '4 times', '8 times'], why: 'The covered area is proportional to the square of the radius, so 4 times.' },
    ] },
  atmos: { title: 'Holding on to an atmosphere', tag: 'Why the Moon has no air',
    body: `Some planets have thick atmospheres and some have almost none, and the difference depends on how well a planet can hold on to gas. Gas molecules move fast depending on temperature: the lighter the molecule and the hotter the gas, the faster. So if a planet's escape speed is not well above the average molecular speed, that gas leaks into space over billions of years. This is why Earth has almost no hydrogen, why the Moon and Mercury, with low escape speeds, have almost no atmosphere, while massive Jupiter holds on to its hydrogen.`,
    deep: String.raw`Molecules in the fast tail of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution escaping from the top of the atmosphere (the exosphere) is called Jeans escape. If the escape speed is about 6 times the average speed, most of the gas remains over the age of the solar system. Mars has a weak magnetic field, so the solar wind stripping its atmosphere also mattered a lot (MAVEN).`,
    s: ['average speed of gas molecules (root mean square)', 'Boltzmann constant', 'temperature', 'mass of one molecule', 'escape speed of the planet'],
    r: 'The hotter and the lighter the molecule, the faster. The escape speed must be about 6 times the average speed for the gas to stay for billions of years. Hydrogen is light, so it leaks away even from Earth.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What is the main reason the Moon has almost no atmosphere?', opts: ['It is too cold', 'Its escape speed is low', 'Its magnetic field is strong', 'It is too far away'], why: 'Its escape speed is only 2.4 km/s, so gas molecules escape easily.' },
      { q: 'When do gas molecules move faster?', opts: ['The hotter and lighter', 'The colder and heavier', 'The higher the pressure'], why: 'The hotter and the lighter the molecule, the faster.' },
      { q: 'Why does Earth have almost no hydrogen?', opts: ['Hydrogen is light and leaks into space', 'Hydrogen dissolved in the ocean', 'There was never any hydrogen'], why: 'Hydrogen is light, so even Earth\'s escape speed cannot hold it.' },
      { q: 'For a gas to remain for billions of years, the escape speed must be about how many times the average speed?', unit: '×', why: 'The escape speed must be about 6 times the average speed for the gas to remain.' },
    ] },
  impact: { title: 'Asteroid impacts and craters', tag: 'The 10 km that ended the dinosaurs',
    body: `Asteroids hit at around 20 km per second, and since kinetic energy is proportional to the square of speed, they release enormous energy for their size. For example, the 2013 Chelyabinsk meteor (20 m across) exploded in the air and shattered windows, and the Chicxulub crater (about 180 km across), made 66 million years ago by a 10 km body, brought about the extinction of the dinosaurs. Below, change the size and the speed.`,
    deep: String.raw`The crater-size formula is a simplified version of scaling laws obtained from experiments and numerical simulations (Collins, Melosh, Marcus 2005). Small bodies break up in the atmosphere, and only rocky or iron bodies tens of meters or larger reach the ground. Near-Earth objects are monitored by NASA's CNEOS, and in 2022 the DART spacecraft actually changed the orbit of the asteroid Dimorphos.`,
    s: ['impact energy', 'mass of the asteroid', 'impact speed', 'crater diameter', 'densities of the asteroid and the ground', 'diameter of the asteroid', 'surface gravity'],
    r: 'Energy is proportional to the square of speed, so even at the same size a faster impact is much stronger. The crater diameter goes as the 0.78 power of the asteroid diameter and the 0.44 power of speed, and it is larger where gravity is weaker.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'If an asteroid of the same size hits twice as fast, how many times more energy does it have?', unit: '×', why: 'E = ½mv², so 4 times.' },
      { q: 'At roughly what speed do asteroids hit, per second?', opts: ['2 km', '20 km', '200 km'], why: 'Asteroids hit at around 20 km per second.' },
      { q: 'Which crater is linked to the extinction of the dinosaurs?', opts: ['Chicxulub', 'Chelyabinsk', 'Tunguska'], why: 'It is the Chicxulub crater, made 66 million years ago by a 10 km body.' },
      { q: 'If the same asteroid hits a body with weaker gravity, the crater is', opts: ['larger', 'smaller', 'the same'], why: 'Crater diameter is larger where gravity is weaker.' },
    ] },
  sun: { title: 'The Sun\'s energy and structure', tag: 'Four million tonnes become light every second',
    body: `The Sun gives off light and heat through hydrogen fusion in its core. At the center (15 million K), four hydrogen nuclei combine into one helium nucleus, and 0.7% of the mass turns into energy. The scale is enormous: the Sun burns about 600 million tonnes of hydrogen per second, and 4 million tonnes of that become light. The energy made in the core is absorbed and re-emitted countless times in the radiative zone, taking tens of thousands of years to reach the surface, but from the surface to Earth it takes only 8 minutes 20 seconds.`,
    deep: String.raw`Protons repel each other electrically, but quantum tunneling lets them fuse with some probability (the pp chain). In the radiative zone a photon's mean free path ℓ is short, so it takes a random walk of <span class="tex">N \approx (R/\ell)^2</span> steps. Solar neutrinos skip all this and come straight out, and underground detectors have directly confirmed the core reactions with them.`,
    s: ['hydrogen nucleus, helium nucleus', 'positron', 'electron neutrino', 'amount of lost mass converted into energy'],
    r: 'Four hydrogen nuclei are 0.7% heavier than one helium nucleus, and the difference comes out as 26.7 MeV of energy. Mass is multiplied by the square of the speed of light, so even a tiny mass becomes a large energy.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'In solar fusion, what percentage of the hydrogen mass becomes energy?', unit: '%', why: 'Going from four hydrogen to one helium loses about 0.7% of the mass.' },
      { q: 'In solar fusion, four hydrogen nuclei become one of what?', opts: ['Helium', 'Carbon', 'Iron'], why: 'Four hydrogen nuclei combine into one helium nucleus.' },
      { q: 'How long does light from the Sun\'s surface take to reach Earth?', opts: ['8 minutes 20 seconds', 'Tens of thousands of years', '1.3 seconds'], why: 'From the surface to Earth takes 8 minutes 20 seconds.' },
      { q: 'Of the hydrogen the Sun burns each second, about how many ten-thousand tonnes become light?', unit: '× 10,000 tonnes', why: 'Of about 600 million tonnes, 4 million tonnes turn into energy.' },
    ] },
  starbirth: { title: 'The birth of stars', tag: 'When a cold, dense gas cloud collapses',
    body: `Stars are born in interstellar molecular clouds. The thermal motion (pressure) of the gas pushes a cloud apart while gravity pulls it together; when gravity wins, the cloud collapses and clumps. The mass at that boundary is the <b>Jeans mass</b>. The colder and denser the cloud, the smaller the Jeans mass, so even small clumps can collapse. This is why stars are born in groups from one cloud, as in the Orion Nebula and the Pleiades.`,
    deep: String.raw`Starting from the virial theorem <span class="tex">2K + U = 0</span> and comparing the thermal energy <span class="tex">\tfrac32 NkT</span> with the gravitational energy <span class="tex">-\tfrac35 GM^2/R</span> gives the Jeans condition. A collapsing protostar forms a disk because of its angular momentum, and planets form in that disk (the disk of HL Tauri imaged by ALMA).`,
    s: ['Jeans mass: a cloud heavier than this collapses on its own', 'Boltzmann constant', 'temperature of the cloud', 'gravitational constant', 'average mass of one gas molecule', 'density of the cloud'],
    r: 'The first bracket is the support from thermal motion; the second is the density. The colder (smaller T) and the denser (larger ρ), the smaller the clumps that can collapse into stars.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'If a molecular cloud gets colder, the Jeans mass', opts: ['gets larger', 'gets smaller', 'stays the same'], why: 'M_J ∝ T^{3/2}, so the colder, the smaller the clumps that can collapse.' },
      { q: 'Where are stars born?', opts: ['In interstellar molecular clouds', 'Inside black holes', 'On planetary surfaces'], why: 'Stars are born in interstellar molecular clouds.' },
      { q: 'Which pairing correctly gives what spreads a cloud and what gathers it?', opts: ['Thermal motion (pressure) - gravity', 'Gravity - thermal motion', 'Magnetic field - light'], why: 'Thermal motion spreads the cloud and gravity gathers it.' },
      { q: 'If a cloud gets denser, the Jeans mass', opts: ['gets smaller', 'gets larger', 'does not change'], why: 'The denser (larger ρ), the smaller the Jeans mass, so small clumps can collapse.' },
    ] },
  nucleo: { title: 'The origin of elements and supernovae', tag: 'We are made of stardust',
    body: `Where did the carbon in our bodies and the iron in our blood come from? The Big Bang made only hydrogen and helium (and a little lithium); everything from carbon and oxygen up to iron was made by fusion inside stars. But iron has the largest binding energy per nucleon, so beyond it fusion releases no energy. So elements heavier than iron formed another way: rapid neutron capture (the r-process) in supernova explosions and neutron star collisions, and slow capture (the s-process) inside old giant stars.`,
    deep: String.raw`The binding energy curve can be roughly drawn with the semi-empirical mass formula <span class="tex">B = a_vA - a_sA^{2/3} - a_c\frac{Z^2}{A^{1/3}} - a_a\frac{(A-2Z)^2}{A} \pm \delta</span>. When an iron core exceeds 1.4 M☉, it collapses in less than a second into a core-collapse supernova. In the 2017 neutron star collision (GW170817), signs of newly made r-process elements such as gold and platinum were observed.`,
    s: ['binding energy per nucleon: average energy needed to remove one particle from the nucleus', 'iron-56 nucleus'],
    r: 'The larger this value, the more tightly bound the nucleus. It peaks near iron, so light elements release energy when fused and heavy elements when split.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What is the heaviest element that can release energy by fusion?', opts: ['Carbon', 'Oxygen', 'Iron', 'Uranium'], why: 'Because the binding energy per nucleon is largest near iron.' },
      { q: 'Which elements did the Big Bang make?', opts: ['Hydrogen and helium (a little lithium)', 'Carbon and oxygen', 'Iron and gold'], why: 'The Big Bang made only hydrogen, helium and a little lithium.' },
      { q: 'Which is NOT a process that makes elements heavier than iron?', opts: ['The r-process', 'The s-process', 'Hydrogen fusion'], why: 'Elements heavier than iron form by rapid (r) or slow (s) neutron capture.' },
      { q: 'For heavy elements to release energy, they must be', opts: ['fused', 'split', 'cooled'], why: 'Binding energy peaks near iron, so heavy elements release energy when split.' },
    ] },
  darkmatter: { title: 'Galaxy rotation curves and dark matter', tag: 'Much heavier than it looks',
    body: `If a galaxy contained only stars and gas, its outer stars should orbit slowly, like the outer planets of the solar system. Instead, flat rotation curves are observed, with nearly the same speed all the way out. This means invisible mass surrounds the galaxy and keeps increasing outward, the most direct evidence for dark matter. This was established by Vera Rubin in the 1970s.`,
    deep: String.raw`A flat curve means <span class="tex">M(\lt r) \propto r</span>, that is, a halo with density <span class="tex">\rho \propto r^{-2}</span>. Gravitational lensing by galaxy clusters, fluctuations in the cosmic microwave background and the Bullet Cluster give the same conclusion. About 27% of the total energy of the universe is dark matter and 5% ordinary matter. What it is remains unknown.`,
    s: ['rotation speed at distance r from the center', 'gravitational constant', 'total mass inside r'],
    r: 'On a circular orbit gravity balances centrifugal force, so the rotation speed is set by the mass inside. For the speed to stay constant outward, the inner mass must keep growing as r grows, but there are not that many visible stars.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What does a flat rotation curve say about a galaxy\'s mass distribution?', opts: ['It is concentrated at the center', 'Mass keeps increasing outward', 'There is no mass'], why: 'If v is constant, M(&lt;r) ∝ r, so invisible mass increases outward.' },
      { q: 'Which scientist established flat rotation curves?', opts: ['Vera Rubin', 'Hubble', 'Kepler'], why: 'Vera Rubin established them in the 1970s.' },
      { q: 'If there were only stars and gas, how should the outer stars of a galaxy orbit?', opts: ['Slowly', 'Fast', 'The same as the inner ones'], why: 'They should orbit slowly, like the outer planets of the solar system.' },
      { q: 'On a circular orbit, what sets the rotation speed?', opts: ['The mass inside that radius', 'The color of the stars', 'The brightness of the galaxy'], why: 'Gravity balances centrifugal force, so the rotation speed is set by the inner mass.' },
    ] },
  distladder: { title: 'The cosmic distance ladder', tag: 'One rung farther at a time',
    body: `No single method can measure cosmic distances all the way out. So several methods are linked like rungs, with each closer rung calibrating the next; this is the cosmic distance ladder. For example, the solar system is measured by radar and nearby stars by parallax. Cepheid variables calibrated with those distances (the period-luminosity relation) measure nearby galaxies, Type Ia supernovae calibrated with Cepheids measure distant galaxies, and beyond that Hubble's law is used. See below how far each rung reaches.`,
    deep: String.raw`Henrietta Leavitt (1912) discovered the period-luminosity relation in Cepheids of the Small Magellanic Cloud. Type Ia supernovae are white dwarfs exploding near the Chandrasekhar limit, so their peak brightness is nearly constant (M ≈ −19.3). Errors in each rung pass to the next, so the debate over the Hubble constant is also a question of calibrating this ladder.`,
    s: [],
    r: 'It is a ladder in which the method for nearby distances calibrates the next method. For example, Cepheids whose distances are known from parallax calibrate the period-luminosity relation, and that relation measures the distances of Cepheids in distant galaxies.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which rung is used to measure the distance to the Andromeda galaxy?', opts: ['Radar', 'Parallax', 'Cepheid variables', 'Hubble\'s law'], why: '770,000 pc is too far for parallax and too close for Hubble\'s law, so Cepheids are used (Hubble, 1924).' },
      { q: 'How are distances within the solar system measured?', opts: ['Radar', 'Type Ia supernovae', 'Hubble\'s law'], why: 'The solar system is measured by radar.' },
      { q: 'How are distances to nearby stars measured?', opts: ['Parallax', 'Hubble\'s law', 'Type Ia supernovae'], why: 'Nearby stars are measured by parallax.' },
      { q: 'Which rung is used to calibrate the brightness of Type Ia supernovae?', opts: ['Cepheid variables', 'Radar', 'Hubble\'s law'], why: 'Type Ia supernovae calibrated with Cepheids measure distant galaxies.' },
    ] },
  spaceweather: { title: 'Space weather: the solar wind and Earth\'s magnetosphere', tag: 'An invisible shield around Earth',
    body: `Space weather is the way solar activity changes the environment around Earth. The Sun constantly blows out the solar wind, a stream of charged particles at hundreds of kilometers per second, and Earth's magnetic field pushes it back, forming a boundary (the magnetopause) about 10 Earth radii away on the day side. But when a solar eruption (a coronal mass ejection) strengthens the solar wind, this boundary is pushed toward Earth. If it comes inside geostationary orbit (6.6 Earth radii), satellites are exposed directly to the solar wind, and auroras reach low latitudes.`,
    deep: String.raw`The boundary is where the dynamic pressure of the solar wind <span class="tex">\rho v^2</span> equals Earth's magnetic pressure <span class="tex">B^2/2\mu_0</span> (for a dipole, <span class="tex">\propto r^{-6}</span>). Big magnetic storms like the 1859 Carrington event can damage power grids and satellites, so NOAA and the Korean Space Weather Center issue space weather forecasts. During the May 2024 storm, auroras were photographed even in Korea.`,
    s: ['distance to the magnetopause (in Earth radii)', 'Earth\'s equatorial surface magnetic field (about 3.1×10⁻⁵ T)', 'permeability of free space', 'density of the solar wind', 'speed of the solar wind'],
    r: 'The boundary is where the pressure pushed by the solar wind (ρv²) equals the pressure held by Earth\'s magnetic field. The field weakens as the cube of distance, hence the 1/6 power. The solar wind pressure must grow 64 times for the boundary to come in to half its distance.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'When the solar wind strengthens, what happens to the magnetopause relative to Earth?', opts: ['It moves away', 'It comes closer', 'It stays the same'], why: 'A larger dynamic pressure ρv² pushes the balance point toward Earth.' },
      { q: 'The solar wind is a stream of what?', opts: ['Charged particles', 'Neutral gas molecules', 'Ice grains'], why: 'The solar wind is a stream of charged particles at hundreds of kilometers per second.' },
      { q: 'Normally, about how many Earth radii away is the day-side magnetopause?', unit: 'R⊕', why: 'Earth\'s magnetic field pushes back the solar wind, forming a boundary at about 10 Earth radii.' },
      { q: 'To bring the magnetopause in to half its distance, how many times must the solar wind pressure grow?', opts: ['2 times', '8 times', '64 times'], why: 'The boundary distance goes inversely as the 1/6 power of pressure, so 2⁶ = 64 times is needed.' },
    ] },
  vanallen: { title: 'The Van Allen belts', tag: 'Radiation trapped by Earth\'s magnetic field',
    body: `The Van Allen belts are two layers of radiation where high-energy particles captured by Earth's magnetic field collect. They were first discovered in 1958 by Explorer 1, the first American satellite. These particles are trapped bouncing between the north and south along magnetic field lines, so spacecraft and satellites passing through receive strong radiation. This damages electronics, so satellites choose orbits that avoid these regions. For example, the International Space Station orbits below them, and the Apollo spacecraft passed through quickly to reduce exposure.`,
    deep: String.raw`Charged particles spiral around field lines (gyration), bounce back near the poles (mirror points) as they travel north and south, and drift around Earth. Over the South Atlantic the magnetic field is weak, so the inner belt dips low (the South Atlantic Anomaly), and the International Space Station's radiation dose rises when it passes there.`,
    s: ['Earth\'s radius (6,371 km)'],
    r: 'This gives the position of the radiation belts in Earth radii. 1.2-3 R⊕ is about 1,300-13,000 km above the surface, and 4-7 R⊕ is about 19,000-38,000 km.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Where is the International Space Station (about 420 km) relative to the Van Allen belts?', opts: ['Inside the inner belt', 'Inside the outer belt', 'Below both belts'], why: 'The inner belt starts at about 1.2 Earth radii (a little over 1,000 km up), so the ISS is below it. It only briefly grazes it in the South Atlantic Anomaly.' },
      { q: 'How many layers do the Van Allen belts have?', unit: 'layers', why: 'The Van Allen belts are two layers of radiation.' },
      { q: 'Which satellite first discovered the Van Allen belts?', opts: ['Explorer 1', 'Sputnik 1', 'Voyager 1'], why: 'Explorer 1, the first American satellite, discovered them in 1958.' },
      { q: 'Roughly how high above the surface is the outer radiation belt (4-7 R⊕)?', opts: ['1,300-13,000 km', '19,000-38,000 km', '100-400 km'], why: '4-7 R⊕ is about 19,000-38,000 km above the surface.' },
    ] },
  decay: { title: 'Orbital decay and atmospheric reentry', tag: 'Low-orbit satellites eventually fall',
    body: `A satellite does not orbit forever once launched; it slowly comes down. Even at a few hundred kilometers there is very thin air, so the satellite constantly feels drag and descends. As it goes lower the air gets thicker, so it falls faster and faster until it finally enters the atmosphere and burns up. That is why the International Space Station raises its altitude with its engines every few months. Also, when solar activity is high the upper atmosphere swells, making the air at the same height denser, so satellites fall faster.`,
    deep: String.raw`Atmospheric density falls with height roughly as <span class="tex">\rho = \rho_0 e^{-(h-h_0)/H}</span>, where the scale height H is tens of kilometers. In February 2022, 38 Starlink satellites were launched on a day when a geomagnetic storm had swollen the atmosphere, and they fell without reaching their orbit. Above 1,000 km objects stay for hundreds to thousands of years, which led to rules for bringing down spent satellites (the 25-year rule, now 5 years).`,
    s: ['orbital radius (from Earth\'s center)', 'Earth\'s gravitational parameter GM', 'atmospheric density at that height', 'drag coefficient (about 2.2 for satellites)', 'cross-section divided by mass: larger for light, wide objects'],
    r: 'The rate at which the orbital radius shrinks is proportional to air density and to "lightness for its area". As height drops, ρ grows sharply, so the fall gets faster and faster.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'When solar activity is high, a low-orbit satellite', opts: ['stays up longer', 'falls faster', 'is unaffected'], why: 'The heated upper atmosphere swells, increasing the density at the same height.' },
      { q: 'Why does a satellite at a few hundred kilometers come down?', opts: ['Drag from very thin air', 'The Sun\'s pull', 'The Moon\'s tides'], why: 'Thin air constantly produces drag, so the satellite comes down.' },
      { q: 'What does the International Space Station do every few months?', opts: ['Raises its altitude with engines', 'Changes its orbital plane', 'Discards solar panels'], why: 'It raises its altitude every few months to counter orbital decay.' },
      { q: 'Why does a satellite fall faster the lower it gets?', opts: ['Air density ρ increases sharply', 'Gravity disappears', 'The satellite gets lighter'], why: 'At lower heights the air is thicker, so decay speeds up.' },
    ] },
  debris: { title: 'Space debris and Kessler syndrome', tag: 'Broken pieces break more',
    body: `Space debris is objects such as dead satellites, rocket remains and fragments orbiting Earth. Around Earth there are over 30,000 tracked objects larger than 10 cm alone, and an estimated one million larger than 1 cm. They collide at 10 km per second, so their kinetic energy is large and even small pieces can destroy satellites. A bigger problem is Kessler syndrome, a chain in which fragments from one collision cause further collisions; if it happens, that altitude becomes unusable for a long time. In fact, the 2009 collision of Iridium 33 and Kosmos 2251 and China's 2007 anti-satellite test created large clouds of debris.`,
    deep: String.raw`With spatial density n (per km³), cross-section σ and relative speed v, the chance an object is hit in a year is <span class="tex">n\sigma v\,t</span>. Altitudes near 800 km are the most crowded. Raising spent satellites to graveyard orbits or bringing them down into the atmosphere, and technologies to capture and remove debris (active removal), are being studied.`,
    s: ['spatial density of debris (number per km³)', 'cross-section of the satellite', 'relative collision speed', 'number of satellites at that altitude'],
    r: 'The volume a satellite sweeps in one second is σ × v. The chance a fragment is in that volume is proportional to n, so the collision rate is the product of the three, and N satellites make it N times larger.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Why is a 1 cm aluminum piece hitting at 10 km/s so dangerous?', opts: ['It is heavy', 'Its kinetic energy is large', 'It is hot'], why: 'In ½mv² the speed is very large, so even a small piece has about the energy of a hand grenade.' },
      { q: 'About how many tracked pieces of debris larger than 10 cm are there, at least?', opts: ['3,000', '30,000', '3 million'], why: 'There are over 30,000 tracked objects larger than 10 cm.' },
      { q: 'What is the chain in which collision fragments cause more collisions?', opts: ['Kessler syndrome', 'The Oberth effect', 'The Roche limit'], why: 'This chain is Kessler syndrome.' },
      { q: 'If the number of satellites at the same altitude doubles, the total collision rate', opts: ['stays the same', 'doubles', 'quadruples'], why: 'With N satellites the collision rate is N times larger.' },
    ] },
  gps: { title: 'GPS and relativity', tag: 'Without correction, 11 km off per day',
    body: `For the GPS in your smartphone to give an accurate position, even relativity has to be taken into account. The atomic clocks on GPS satellites, 20,000 km up, feel two relativistic effects. They run slow because they move fast (special relativity, −7 μs per day) and run fast because they sit where gravity is weaker (general relativity, +45 μs per day). Together they run about 38 μs fast per day, and since light travels 300 m in 1 μs, without correction positions would drift about 11 km per day. So GPS clocks are deliberately set slightly slow before launch.`,
    deep: String.raw`In the weak-field approximation, the ratio of proper time is <span class="tex">d\tau/dt \approx 1 + \Phi/c^2 - v^2/2c^2</span>. There is an orbital height (about 3,200 km) where the two effects are equal and cancel. The Korean Positioning System (KPS) makes the same correction.`,
    s: ['fraction by which the satellite clock runs faster than a ground clock', 'Earth\'s mass expressed as a length (about 4.4 mm)', 'orbital radius of the satellite', 'orbital speed of the satellite', 'speed of light'],
    r: 'The first term is the effect of a clock running fast higher up where gravity is weaker (general relativity); the second is a moving clock running slow (special relativity). At GPS height the first term is larger, so overall the clock runs fast.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About how many microseconds per day does a GPS satellite clock run faster than one on the ground?', unit: 'μs', why: 'The gravity effect is +45.7 μs and the speed effect −7.2 μs, for a total of about +38.5 μs.' },
      { q: 'About how high are GPS satellites?', opts: ['420 km', '20,000 km', '380,000 km'], why: 'GPS satellites orbit at about 20,000 km.' },
      { q: 'Which effect makes a clock run slow because it moves fast?', opts: ['Special relativity, −7 μs per day', 'General relativity, +45 μs per day', 'The Doppler effect'], why: 'Fast motion makes it run 7 μs slow per day, a special-relativity effect.' },
      { q: 'Without correcting the clocks, about how many km per day would positions drift?', unit: 'km', why: '38 μs × 300 m/μs ≈ 11 km.' },
    ] },
  gw: { title: 'Gravitational waves', tag: 'Spacetime trembles',
    body: `When two heavy bodies orbit each other, they give off gravitational waves, ripples in spacetime, lose energy and spiral closer. The closer they get, the faster they orbit, so just before merging there is a 'chirp' signal whose frequency shoots up. In 2015 LIGO first detected one from the collision of two black holes (36 and 29 times the Sun's mass) 1.3 billion light-years away; the signal was so small that it changed the length of a 4 km arm on Earth by one thousandth of a proton's diameter.`,
    deep: String.raw`The chirp mass <span class="tex">\mathcal M = (m_1m_2)^{3/5}/(m_1+m_2)^{1/5}</span> sets the rate of frequency change: <span class="tex">\dot f = \frac{96}{5}\pi^{8/3}\left(\frac{G\mathcal M}{c^3}\right)^{5/3}f^{11/3}</span>. The shrinking orbital period of the binary pulsar discovered in 1974 (Hulse-Taylor) matched the prediction of general relativity exactly, giving indirect evidence for gravitational waves. In the 2017 neutron star collision, light (gamma rays and a kilonova) was observed as well.`,
    s: ['frequency of the gravitational waves', 'time remaining until the merger', 'chirp mass: a combination of the two masses', 'gravitational constant and speed of light'],
    r: 'As the merger approaches (small τ), the frequency shoots up as τ^−3/8. The heavier the pair, the lower the frequency at the same moment. So the shape of the signal alone reveals the mass.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Just before two black holes merge, the gravitational-wave frequency', opts: ['falls', 'rises', 'stays constant'], why: 'As they get closer they orbit faster, giving a chirp signal whose frequency shoots up.' },
      { q: 'Gravitational waves are ripples in what?', opts: ['Spacetime', 'Seawater', 'The electromagnetic field'], why: 'Gravitational waves are ripples in spacetime.' },
      { q: 'Which facility first detected gravitational waves?', opts: ['LIGO', 'EHT', 'TESS'], why: 'In 2015 LIGO first detected them from the collision of two black holes.' },
      { q: 'At the same moment, which has the lower frequency, a heavy pair or a light pair?', opts: ['The heavy pair', 'The light pair', 'The same'], why: 'The heavier the pair, the lower the frequency at the same moment.' },
    ] },
  scale: { title: 'The size of the universe', tag: 'Distances measured with light',
    body: `Distances in the universe are so huge that kilometers give no feel for them, so they are expressed as the time light takes to travel. Light is the fastest thing, at 300,000 km per second, so this time becomes a ruler for distance. For example, light takes 1.3 seconds to reach the Moon, 8 minutes 20 seconds to the Sun, about 23 hours to Voyager 1, and 4.2 years to the nearest star. Going further, the Milky Way is 100,000 light-years across, and the Andromeda galaxy is 2.5 million light-years away. So all the starlight in the night sky is light from the past.`,
    deep: String.raw`The astronomical unit (AU, the mean Earth-Sun distance of 149.6 million km), the light-year (9.46 trillion km) and the parsec (3.26 light-years) are used as suits the situation. The radius of the observable universe is about 46.5 billion light-years, larger than the age of the universe (13.8 billion years), because space stretched while the light was traveling.`,
    s: ['distance', 'speed of light (about 300,000 km per second)', 'time light takes to travel that distance'],
    r: 'Light circles Earth seven and a half times in one second. Cosmic distances are those that take even that light years or millions of years. So the farther away we look, the further into the past we see.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'How long does sunlight take to reach Earth?', opts: ['8 seconds', 'About 8 minutes', 'About 8 hours'], why: '150 million km ÷ 300,000 km/s ≈ 500 seconds ≈ 8 minutes 20 seconds.' },
      { q: 'How long does light take to reach the Moon?', opts: ['1.3 seconds', '8 minutes 20 seconds', '4.2 years'], why: 'It takes 1.3 seconds to the Moon.' },
      { q: 'How long does light take to cross the Milky Way?', opts: ['100,000 years', '2.5 million years', '4.2 years'], why: 'The Milky Way is 100,000 light-years across.' },
      { q: 'About how many ten-thousands of years ago did the light from the Andromeda galaxy set out?', unit: '× 10,000 years', why: 'Andromeda is 2.5 million light-years away, so its light is that old.' },
    ] },
  bluesky: { title: 'Why the sky is blue and sunsets are red', tag: 'Air scatters light',
    body: `Why is the sky blue on a clear day, and why are sunsets red? The answer is Rayleigh scattering, in which air molecules scatter sunlight. Sunlight is white light mixing many colors, and air molecules scatter short-wavelength blue light more than 5 times as well as red light. So wherever you look in the sky, scattered blue light reaches your eyes. On the other hand, when the Sun is low, its light passes through tens of times more air, all the blue is scattered out along the way, and the remaining orange and red light makes the sunset. On the Moon, with no air, there are no molecules to scatter light, so the sky is black even in daytime.`,
    deep: String.raw`For particles much smaller than the wavelength, like molecules, scattering strength is proportional to <span class="tex">\lambda^{-4}</span>. Large particles such as water droplets or dust scatter all colors alike (Mie scattering), so clouds look white. On Mars red dust makes the daytime sky pinkish, and the sunsets are blue instead.`,
    s: ['intensity of scattered light', 'wavelength of light (blue about 450 nm, red about 700 nm)'],
    r: 'Halve the wavelength and the scattered intensity is 2⁴ = 16 times. Blue light (450 nm) scatters about 5.9 times better than red light (700 nm). So the whole sky fills with blue light, and sunset light that passed through a long stretch of air keeps only the red.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Light with half the wavelength scatters how many times more?', unit: '×', why: '(1/2)⁻⁴ = 16 times.' },
      { q: 'Which scattering explains why the sky is blue?', opts: ['Rayleigh scattering', 'Mie scattering', 'Reflection'], why: 'It is Rayleigh scattering of sunlight by air molecules.' },
      { q: 'Why is the sky black on the Moon even in daytime?', opts: ['There is no air to scatter light', 'The Sun is dim', 'The Moon does not rotate'], why: 'There is no air, so there are no molecules to scatter light.' },
      { q: 'Why are sunsets red?', opts: ['Light passes through a long stretch of air and the blue is scattered out', 'The Sun cools down', 'Red light scatters more'], why: 'When the Sun is low, its light passes through a lot of air, the blue drops out and the red remains.' },
    ] },
  aurora: { title: 'Auroras', tag: 'Light given off by the atmosphere',
    body: `Auroras are lights that ripple like curtains in the polar night sky. When electrons from the solar wind pour into the polar atmosphere along Earth's magnetic field lines, they excite oxygen and nitrogen atoms. As the excited atoms return to normal, they give off light of a color fixed for each element. For example, oxygen at 100-200 km glows green (557.7 nm), thin oxygen above 200 km glows red (630 nm), and nitrogen gives purple and pink light along the lower edge. During strong geomagnetic storms, red auroras have even been photographed in the northern sky from Korea (May 2024).`,
    deep: String.raw`The 630 nm red light is a forbidden line that appears only if the excited state lasts about 110 seconds, so it is seen only in the thin upper atmosphere where atoms do not collide with other molecules during that time. The auroral band (the auroral oval) forms around magnetic latitude 65-70°, and it widens toward the equator as storms get stronger.`,
    s: ['energy of one photon', 'Planck constant', 'frequency of the light', 'speed of light', 'wavelength of the light'],
    r: 'Atoms can only move up and down fixed energy steps, so the energy and color of the light they give off is fixed for each element. The greater the energy, the shorter (bluer) the wavelength. The color of an aurora tells you which atoms are glowing at what height.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which element gives the green light of auroras?', opts: ['Nitrogen', 'Oxygen', 'Hydrogen'], why: 'Oxygen atoms at 100-200 km give off green light at 557.7 nm.' },
      { q: 'What particles mainly cause auroras?', opts: ['Electrons from the solar wind', 'Cosmic dust', 'Raindrops'], why: 'Electrons from the solar wind pour into the atmosphere along magnetic field lines.' },
      { q: 'What color does thin oxygen above 200 km give off?', opts: ['Red', 'Green', 'Purple'], why: 'Thin oxygen high up gives off red light at 630 nm.' },
      { q: 'Which has more energy, the green (557.7 nm) or the red (630 nm) auroral light?', opts: ['The green light', 'The red light', 'The same'], why: 'The shorter the wavelength, the greater the energy, so the green light has more.' },
    ] },
  meteor: { title: 'Comets and meteor showers', tag: 'Shooting stars from burning dust',
    body: `Meteor showers, when many meteors streak across the night sky at once, come from dust left by comets. A comet is a lump of ice and dust; as it nears the Sun its ice vaporizes, forming a tail and shedding dust along its orbit. Earth passes through that dust trail on the same date every year, so the shower returns at the same time every year. Grains the size of sand plunge into the atmosphere at 11-72 km per second and burn at 80-120 km, leaving streaks of light. Dust meeting Earth head-on (the Leonids) is fast, and dust catching up from behind is slow.`,
    deep: String.raw`<span class="tex">v_\infty</span> is the relative speed before being pulled in by Earth's gravity, and Earth's escape speed of 11.2 km/s is added on top. The maximum relative speed is 72 km/s, Earth's orbital speed of 30 km/s plus the solar system escape speed of 42 km/s head-on. Meteor showers are named after the constellation containing the point from which the meteors seem to spread out (the radiant).`,
    s: ['speed entering the atmosphere', 'speed relative to Earth before being pulled in by Earth\'s gravity', 'Earth\'s escape speed, 11.2 km/s'],
    r: 'Earth\'s gravity pulls the grain in and speeds it up by that much. However slowly it approaches, it falls at least at 11.2 km/s, and since energy goes as speed squared, faster meteors burn much brighter.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Roughly what is the slowest speed at which a meteor enters the atmosphere?', opts: ['1 km/s', '11 km/s', '72 km/s'], why: 'Pulled in by Earth\'s gravity, it reaches at least the escape speed of 11.2 km/s.' },
      { q: 'Meteor showers come from dust left by what?', opts: ['Comets', 'The Moon', 'Artificial satellites'], why: 'They happen when Earth passes through a dust trail shed by a comet along its orbit.' },
      { q: 'At about what height do meteors burn?', opts: ['10-20 km', '80-120 km', '400-500 km'], why: 'Grains burn at 80-120 km, leaving streaks of light.' },
      { q: 'Why do meteor showers return at the same time every year?', opts: ['Earth passes the dust trail on the same date each year', 'The comet comes on the same date each year', 'Because of the Moon\'s phase'], why: 'Earth passes through that dust trail on the same date every year.' },
    ] },
  drake: { title: 'How many alien civilizations are there?', tag: 'The Drake equation',
    body: `In 1961 Frank Drake wrote a one-line formula to work out "how many civilizations in our galaxy could be sending signals right now". This is the Drake equation. It multiplies, in turn, the rate at which stars form, the chance a star has planets, the number of planets suitable for life, the chance life arises, the chance it becomes intelligent, the chance it develops technology to send signals, and how long such a civilization lasts. Of these, the first three are now fairly well known from observations, but nobody yet knows the last four.`,
    deep: String.raw`Thanks to Kepler and TESS, these have been narrowed to <span class="tex">f_p \approx 1</span> (most stars have planets) and <span class="tex">n_e \sim 0.1\text{–}0.4</span>. The question of why we see no one when the universe is so large and old is called the Fermi paradox.`,
    s: ['number of civilizations in our galaxy that can send signals now', 'number of new stars formed per year', 'fraction of stars with planets', 'number of planets suitable for life per such star', 'chance life arises on such a planet', 'chance life becomes intelligent', 'chance of developing technology to send signals far away', 'how long such a civilization lasts (years)'],
    r: 'The product of the earlier terms is "how many communicating civilizations arise per year"; multiplying by the lifetime L gives how many exist at the same time now. Because it is a product, if even one term is very small, N drops a lot.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which term of the Drake equation is best known from observation?', opts: ['f_p (fraction of stars with planets)', 'f_i (chance of intelligent life)', 'L (lifetime of a civilization)'], why: 'Exoplanet surveys have shown that most stars have planets.' },
      { q: 'In what year was the Drake equation written?', opts: ['1961', '1929', '2015'], why: 'Frank Drake wrote it in 1961.' },
      { q: 'How does the Drake equation combine its terms?', opts: ['It multiplies them', 'It adds them', 'It averages them'], why: 'It multiplies the terms in turn.' },
      { q: 'If one term is very small, N', opts: ['drops a lot', 'hardly changes', 'grows'], why: 'Because it is a product, even one small term makes N drop a lot.' },
    ] },
  calendar: { title: 'The cosmic calendar', tag: '13.8 billion years as one year',
    body: `The cosmic calendar, made popular by Carl Sagan, shrinks the roughly 13.8-billion-year history of the universe into one year. Setting the Big Bang at midnight on January 1 and the present at midnight on December 31 lets you compare vast spans of time at a glance. On this calendar the Milky Way forms around March and the solar system in early September. Dinosaurs appear on December 25 and vanish on the 30th, and humans appear around 10:30 p.m. on December 31. All of recorded history fits in the last 14 seconds.`,
    deep: String.raw`One second on the calendar is really about 440 years, and one day about 37.8 million years. At this scale, life appeared on Earth fairly quickly (late September), but complex multicellular life took a very long time to arrive (mid-December).`,
    s: ['actual time since the Big Bang', 'date on the one-year calendar'],
    r: 'It is a proportion that shrinks 13.8 billion years into 365 days. A calendar day is about 37.8 million years, an hour about 1.6 million years, and a second about 440 years.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'On the cosmic calendar, on which day do humans appear?', opts: ['December 1', 'December 25', 'December 31'], why: '300,000 years is about 11 minutes on the calendar, late on December 31.' },
      { q: 'On the cosmic calendar, when is the Big Bang?', opts: ['Midnight, January 1', 'Midnight, December 31', 'Early September'], why: 'The Big Bang is set at midnight on January 1.' },
      { q: 'On the cosmic calendar, when does the solar system form?', opts: ['Around March', 'Early September', 'December 25'], why: 'The solar system forms in early September.' },
      { q: 'About how many ten-thousands of years is one day on the cosmic calendar?', unit: '× 10,000 years', why: '13.8 billion years ÷ 365 ≈ 37.8 million years.' },
    ] },
  microg: { title: 'Why is a space station weightless?', tag: 'Falling without end',
    body: `Why do astronauts float inside a space station? Not because there is no gravity. In fact, at the International Space Station (about 420 km up) gravity is about 89% of that on the ground, almost unchanged. But the station and the astronauts are constantly falling toward Earth together. At the same time they fly sideways at 7.7 km per second, so as they fall, Earth's curved surface drops away just as much and they never hit the ground. This is an orbit, and people falling together feel no weight (free fall).`,
    deep: String.raw`Strictly speaking it is not "zero gravity" but "microgravity". Away from the station's center, tidal effects and air drag leave a very small acceleration (around 10⁻⁶ g). Staying a long time weakens bones and muscles, and body fluids shift toward the head, making the face puffy.`,
    s: ['gravitational acceleration at height h', 'gravitational acceleration at the surface, 9.8 m/s²', 'Earth\'s radius, 6,371 km', 'height above the surface'],
    r: 'Gravity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from Earth\'s center. 420 km is small compared with Earth\'s radius, so gravity drops by only 11%. Astronauts float not because there is no gravity but because they fall together.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'At a height of 420 km, gravity is what percentage of that on the ground?', unit: '%', why: '(6371/6791)² ≈ 0.885.' },
      { q: 'Why do astronauts in a space station float?', opts: ['There is no gravity', 'They are in free fall together with the station', 'The air holds them up'], why: 'People falling together feel no weight.' },
      { q: 'About how many km per second does the International Space Station fly sideways?', unit: 'km/s', why: 'The station flies sideways at 7.7 km per second.' },
      { q: 'Why does the station never hit the ground?', opts: ['As it falls, Earth\'s curved surface drops away just as much', 'Its engines keep pushing it up', 'Gravity acts upward'], why: 'It flies sideways so fast that the surface drops away as much as it falls.' },
    ] },
  eht: { title: 'An Earth-sized telescope that imaged a black hole', tag: 'The Event Horizon Telescope',
    body: `Imaging a black hole's shadow needs a telescope the size of Earth, and the Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) built exactly that by linking radio telescopes around the world. The smallest angle a telescope can distinguish (its resolution) is proportional to the wavelength of light divided by the telescope's diameter. So at the same wavelength, making the diameter 10 times larger makes the smallest angle 10 times smaller, letting you see things 10 times smaller, and likewise a shorter wavelength gives a sharper view. But the shadow of the black hole at the center of galaxy M87 is just 40 microarcseconds, the size of a doughnut on the Moon. To see it, eight radio telescope sites observed at the same time, acting like a telescope with diameter D ≈ 10,000 km, and produced the first black hole image in 2019 and an image of the Milky Way's central black hole in 2022. Korea's KVN telescopes also take part in follow-up observations.`,
    deep: String.raw`This is very long baseline interferometry (VLBI): the radio waves received at each telescope are recorded on hard disks along with atomic clock times and then combined on a supercomputer. The shadow's diameter is about 5.2 times the Schwarzschild radius, so its size gives the black hole's mass (M87*: 6.5 billion times the Sun).`,
    s: ['smallest angle that can be distinguished (resolution)', 'wavelength of the observed light (1.3 mm for the EHT)', 'telescope diameter (for an interferometer, the distance between the two farthest telescopes)'],
    r: 'At the same wavelength, the bigger the telescope, and with the same telescope, the shorter the wavelength, the smaller the details it can distinguish. Radio waves have long wavelengths, so the telescope must be as big as Earth to see a black hole\'s shadow.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'At the same wavelength, if the telescope diameter is made 10 times larger, the resolution angle', opts: ['gets 10 times larger', 'gets 10 times smaller', 'stays the same'], why: 'θ ∝ λ/D, so with D 10 times larger θ is 1/10, distinguishing things 10 times smaller.' },
      { q: 'How did the EHT make an Earth-sized telescope?', opts: ['By linking radio telescopes around the world', 'By building one giant mirror', 'By launching a telescope into space'], why: 'Radio telescopes around the world observed at the same time and were used as one.' },
      { q: 'With the same telescope, if the wavelength gets shorter, the resolution', opts: ['gets sharper', 'gets blurrier', 'stays the same'], why: 'The resolution angle is proportional to wavelength, so shorter wavelengths are sharper.' },
      { q: 'In what year did the EHT release the first black hole image?', opts: ['2019', '2015', '2022'], why: 'It released the M87 black hole image in 2019 and the Milky Way\'s central black hole in 2022.' },
    ] },
  hrdiagram: { title: 'The H-R diagram', tag: 'A map of stars by temperature and brightness · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `A plot of stars by surface temperature (horizontal, hotter to the left) and luminosity (vertical) is called the H-R diagram. Plotted this way, stars do not scatter at random but gather into a few groups, so you can read the kinds of stars and their lives. The band running from upper left to lower right is the <b>main sequence</b>, stars burning hydrogen in their cores; about 90% of stars are here. At upper right are the cool but very bright <b>giants and supergiants</b>, and at lower left the hot but dim <b>white dwarfs</b>. Being brighter at the same temperature means being larger, so the higher a star sits on the H-R diagram, the larger its radius (dotted lines are lines of equal radius). Also, on the main sequence, higher stars are heavier and shorter-lived.`,
    deep: String.raw`Observers use a color-magnitude diagram plotted with color index (B−V) and absolute magnitude. In the H-R diagram of a star cluster, all the stars have the same age, so the point where stars, heaviest first, have peeled off the main sequence (the turnoff point) gives the cluster's age.`,
    s: ['luminosity: total energy a star emits per second', 'radius of the star', 'Stefan-Boltzmann constant', 'surface temperature'],
    r: 'Luminosity is the surface area (4πR²) times the light emitted per unit area (σT⁴). So on the H-R diagram, at the same temperature a higher star is larger, and at the same luminosity a star farther left (hotter) is smaller.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'On the H-R diagram, a star at the same temperature but higher up (brighter) is', opts: ['smaller', 'larger', 'closer'], why: 'L = 4πR²σT⁴, so at the same temperature, brighter means a larger radius. Distance has nothing to do with luminosity.' },
      { q: 'What is on the left of the H-R diagram\'s horizontal axis?', opts: ['The hot side', 'The cool side', 'The bright side'], why: 'The horizontal axis is surface temperature, with hot on the left.' },
      { q: 'Which group contains about 90% of stars?', opts: ['Main-sequence stars', 'Giants', 'White dwarfs'], why: 'About 90% are main-sequence stars burning hydrogen in their cores.' },
      { q: 'What stars are at the lower left of the H-R diagram?', opts: ['Hot but dim white dwarfs', 'Cool, bright supergiants', 'Heavy main-sequence stars'], why: 'The lower left holds hot but dim white dwarfs.' },
    ] },
  spectral: { title: 'Spectral types and star colors', tag: 'OBAFGKM · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `Spread starlight with a prism and dark lines (absorption lines) appear here and there; which element's lines look strong is decided more by the <b>surface temperature</b> than by the star's composition. So stars are sorted from hottest to coolest as O, B, A, F, G, K, M, and each type is divided further with the numbers 0-9. For example, O stars are blue and close to 40,000 K, the Sun is a yellow G2 star (about 5,800 K), and M stars are red and about 3,000 K. Hydrogen absorption lines are strongest in A stars near 10,000 K; in hotter stars hydrogen is ionized, and in cooler stars it is not excited, so the lines weaken.`,
    deep: String.raw`Line strength is set jointly by the ionization of atoms (the Saha equation) and their excitation (the Boltzmann distribution). Adding a luminosity class (I supergiant to V main sequence), the Sun is written G2V and Betelgeuse M1-2Ia.`,
    s: ['wavelength of the strongest light', 'surface temperature (K)'],
    r: 'The hotter the star, the more its strongest light shifts to short (blue) wavelengths, so you can guess its temperature from its color alone. Spectral types divide temperature more precisely using the pattern of absorption lines.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which spectral type has the strongest hydrogen absorption lines?', opts: ['Type O', 'Type A', 'Type M'], why: 'In A stars at about 10,000 K hydrogen is best excited, so the lines are strongest.' },
      { q: 'Which lists the spectral types from hottest to coolest?', opts: ['O, B, A, F, G, K, M', 'M, K, G, F, A, B, O', 'A, B, F, G, K, M, O'], why: 'Stars are sorted from hottest to coolest as O, B, A, F, G, K, M.' },
      { q: 'What is the Sun\'s spectral type?', opts: ['G2', 'O', 'M'], why: 'The Sun is a yellow G2 star (about 5,800 K).' },
      { q: 'Why do hydrogen lines weaken in very hot stars?', opts: ['Hydrogen is ionized', 'There is no hydrogen', 'Hydrogen cannot be excited'], why: 'In hotter stars hydrogen is ionized; in cooler stars it is not excited, so the lines weaken.' },
    ] },
  starstruct: { title: 'The internal structure of stars', tag: 'Fusion and energy transport · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `A star is in <b>hydrostatic equilibrium</b>, with its own gravity pressing inward balanced by the pressure of hot gas pushing outward. Energy made by hydrogen fusion in the core flows outward: in a star like the Sun it leaks out slowly in the inner 70%, the <b>radiative zone</b>, as light is absorbed and re-emitted over and over, while in the outer 30%, the <b>convective zone</b>, gas rises and sinks like boiling water to carry it. By contrast, stars much heavier than the Sun have convective cores and radiative outer layers, and very light red dwarfs are convective throughout. Also, in main-sequence stars heavier than about 2 solar masses, the main fusion process is not the proton-proton chain but the <b>CNO cycle</b>, which uses carbon, nitrogen and oxygen as catalysts.`,
    deep: String.raw`When more energy must flow than radiation can carry, the temperature gradient steepens and convection begins (the Schwarzschild criterion). In heavier stars, where fusion is concentrated in the core, the central energy flow is large, so they have convective cores.`,
    s: ['gas pressure at that depth', 'distance from the center', 'mass inside r', 'density at that depth', 'gravitational constant'],
    r: 'Deeper in, the pressure must be larger to hold up the weight piled above. As long as this equation holds, the star stays in balance, neither growing nor shrinking.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'In the outer 30% of the Sun, how is energy mainly carried?', opts: ['Radiation', 'Convection', 'Conduction'], why: 'The Sun\'s inner 70% is the radiative zone and the outer 30% the convective zone.' },
      { q: 'What is the state in which a star neither grows nor shrinks?', opts: ['Hydrostatic equilibrium', 'Thermal collapse', 'Free fall'], why: 'It is hydrostatic equilibrium, with gravity and pressure balanced.' },
      { q: 'How is energy carried in the inner 70% of the Sun?', opts: ['Radiation', 'Convection', 'Conduction'], why: 'In the inner 70%, the radiative zone, light leaks out by repeated absorption and emission.' },
      { q: 'What is the main fusion process in main-sequence stars heavier than about 2 solar masses?', opts: ['The CNO cycle', 'The proton-proton chain', 'The triple-alpha process'], why: 'The CNO cycle, using carbon, nitrogen and oxygen as catalysts, dominates.' },
    ] },
  exodetect: { title: 'Ways to find exoplanets', tag: 'Radial velocity · transit · microlensing · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `Exoplanets are too faint to see directly, so they are mostly found through their effect on their star. First, in the <b>radial velocity method</b>, the planet pulls on the star so that it wobbles back and forth slightly, and the Doppler effect shifts its spectral lines alternately bluer and redder. The heavier the planet and the closer to the star, the larger the wobble (K), and the easier it is to find. Second, in the <b>transit method</b>, the starlight dims briefly when the planet passes in front of the star, and the fraction of dimming gives the planet's size. Third, in the <b>microlensing method</b>, when a foreground star focuses the light of a background star with its gravity and brightens it, a planet of the foreground star makes a short extra brightening. KMTNet of the Korea Astronomy and Space Science Institute has found many planets this way.`,
    deep: String.raw`Radial velocity gives only <span class="tex">m_p \sin i</span> (the minimum mass), but if a transit is also seen, i ≈ 90°, so both mass and radius are known and the density can be found. Direct imaging blocks the starlight with a coronagraph to photograph young, hot giant planets far from their stars.`,
    s: ['amplitude of the star\'s radial velocity wobble (m/s)', 'orbital period of the planet', 'planet mass × sine of the orbital tilt (minimum mass)', 'mass of the star', 'gravitational constant'],
    r: 'The heavier the planet and the shorter its period (the closer to the star), the more it shakes the star. The same planet shakes a lighter star more.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which planets are easiest to find with the radial velocity method?', opts: ['Heavy planets close to the star', 'Light planets far from the star', 'Planets that are merely large'], why: 'The heavier and closer, the more the star wobbles and the larger the Doppler shift.' },
      { q: 'How are exoplanets mainly found?', opts: ['Through their effect on their star', 'By photographing the planet directly', 'By sending probes to the planet'], why: 'Planets are too faint, so they are mainly found through their effect on the star.' },
      { q: 'Which method did KMTNet use to find many planets?', opts: ['Microlensing', 'Radial velocity', 'Direct imaging'], why: 'KMTNet of the Korea Astronomy and Space Science Institute found them by microlensing.' },
      { q: 'If the same planet orbits a lighter star, the star\'s wobble K', opts: ['gets larger', 'gets smaller', 'stays the same'], why: 'The same planet shakes a lighter star more.' },
    ] },
  galaxytypes: { title: 'Hubble\'s classification of galaxies', tag: 'Elliptical · spiral · barred spiral · irregular · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `Hubble sorted galaxies by their appearance. Different shapes mean different mixes of stars and gas, so this classification tells you about a galaxy's character. First, <b>elliptical galaxies (E)</b> are round or elongated balls without spiral arms; they have many old red stars and little gas, so few new stars form. The numbers 0-7 give how flattened they are. Next, <b>spiral galaxies (S)</b> are disks with a central bulge and spiral arms, with lots of gas and young blue stars in the arms. If a bar crosses the center, it is a <b>barred spiral galaxy (SB)</b>, and our Milky Way is one of these. From a to c, spirals have smaller bulges and more loosely wound arms. Finally, galaxies without a definite shape are <b>irregular galaxies (Irr)</b>, such as the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds.`,
    deep: String.raw`Hubble thought of this sequence as an evolutionary order, but now it is thought that more often spiral galaxies merge and become ellipticals. By color and star formation rate, galaxies split into the "blue cloud" and the "red sequence".`,
    s: ['elliptical galaxy (larger number = flatter)', 'lenticular galaxy', 'spiral galaxy, barred spiral galaxy (a→c: smaller bulge, more open arms)'],
    r: 'This is not a formula but notation for an order by shape. Toward the left, galaxies are round and dominated by old stars; toward the right, they have flat disks with many young stars.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'What kind of galaxy is the Milky Way?', opts: ['Elliptical', 'Barred spiral', 'Irregular'], why: 'It is a spiral galaxy with a bar at its center (about SBbc).' },
      { q: 'What is characteristic of elliptical galaxies?', opts: ['Many old red stars and little gas', 'Many young stars in spiral arms', 'No definite shape'], why: 'Elliptical galaxies have many old red stars and form few new ones.' },
      { q: 'What kind of galaxies are the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds?', opts: ['Irregular', 'Elliptical', 'Barred spiral'], why: 'They are examples of irregular galaxies with no definite shape.' },
      { q: 'In spiral galaxies, going from a to c,', opts: ['the bulge gets smaller and the arms looser', 'the bulge gets bigger and the arms tighter', 'they become ellipticals'], why: 'From a to c the bulge gets smaller and the arms open up more loosely.' },
    ] },
  bigbang: { title: 'Big Bang cosmology and its evidence', tag: 'The universe began hot and small · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `Big Bang cosmology is the theory that the universe has been expanding and cooling from a very hot, dense state about 13.8 billion years ago. It is supported by three major pieces of evidence. First, <b>Hubble's law</b>, in which more distant galaxies recede faster, shows that the universe is expanding. Second, the 2.7 K <b>cosmic microwave background</b> coming from everywhere is light first set free when the universe was 3,000 K, since stretched and cooled. Third, the ratio of ordinary matter by mass, <b>about 75% hydrogen and about 25% helium</b>, matches calculations of fusion in the first three minutes. The steady-state theory (in which matter keeps forming as the universe expands so that the density stays constant), by contrast, could not explain the background radiation and was abandoned.`,
    deep: String.raw`In early nucleosynthesis the neutron-to-proton ratio froze at about 1:7; if all neutrons go into helium-4, the helium mass fraction is <span class="tex">2 \times 1/(1+7) = 0.25</span>. Inflation theory was added to explain why the universe is flat and uniform everywhere.`,
    s: ['mass of helium', 'mass of hydrogen'],
    r: 'Calculations of fusion in the first three minutes predict that about 25% of ordinary matter should be helium, and the observed value matches. Helium made by stars alone could not be this abundant.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'Which is NOT evidence for Big Bang cosmology?', opts: ['The cosmic microwave background', 'A hydrogen-to-helium mass ratio of about 3:1', 'Stellar parallax'], why: 'Parallax is evidence of Earth\'s orbit and a way to measure distance.' },
      { q: 'According to Big Bang cosmology, about how many hundred million years ago did the universe begin?', unit: '× 100 million years', why: 'The universe has been expanding from a hot, dense state for about 13.8 billion years.' },
      { q: 'What fraction of ordinary matter by mass is helium?', opts: ['About 25%', 'About 75%', 'About 5%'], why: 'About 75% hydrogen and 25% helium, matching fusion calculations for the first three minutes.' },
      { q: 'Why was the steady-state theory abandoned?', opts: ['It could not explain the cosmic microwave background', 'It denied Hubble\'s law', 'It could not explain stars'], why: 'The steady-state theory could not explain the background radiation.' },
    ] },
  darkenergy: { title: 'Dark energy and accelerating expansion', tag: '95% of the universe is unknown · High school Earth Science I',
    body: `Type Ia supernovae have nearly the same peak brightness, so they are used as "standard candles" for measuring distance. But in 1998 it turned out that distant Type Ia supernovae were fainter, and so farther, than expected. This means cosmic expansion is not slowing down but has been <b>speeding up</b> for about 5 billion years. The mysterious energy driving this acceleration is called <b>dark energy</b>. Today about 69% of the universe's energy is dark energy, about 26% is <b>dark matter</b>, which gives off no light and shows itself only through gravity, and ordinary matter, which makes up stars, planets and our bodies, is only about 5%.`,
    deep: String.raw`The simplest explanation is energy belonging to space itself (the cosmological constant Λ); its density stays the same as space stretches, so its share grows over time. Recent DESI observations hint that dark energy may change over time, and research continues.`,
    s: ['fraction made up by dark energy', 'fraction of dark matter', 'fraction of ordinary matter'],
    r: 'The three add up to almost 1, so the universe is flat. The matter we know is only 5%.',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'About what fraction of today\'s universe is ordinary matter?', opts: ['5%', '26%', '69%'], why: 'Ordinary matter about 5%, dark matter about 26%, dark energy about 69%.' },
      { q: 'Why are Type Ia supernovae used as standard candles?', opts: ['Their peak brightness is nearly the same', 'They are blue', 'They are nearby'], why: 'Their peak brightness is nearly the same, so they are used to measure distance.' },
      { q: 'About what fraction of today\'s universe is dark energy?', opts: ['69%', '26%', '5%'], why: 'Dark energy about 69%, dark matter about 26%, ordinary matter about 5%.' },
      { q: 'When did cosmic expansion start speeding up?', opts: ['About 5 billion years ago', 'About 13.8 billion years ago', 'About 380,000 years ago'], why: 'The expansion has been speeding up for about 5 billion years.' },
    ] },
  synodic: { title: 'Apparent motion of the planets', tag: 'Synodic period and retrograde motion · High school Earth Science II',
    body: `Planets usually move from west to east among the constellations (prograde motion), but sometimes they go backward from east to west, which is <b>retrograde motion</b>. It happens when Earth overtakes an outer planet (such as Mars) on the inside, just as a train you pass seems to move backward. So an outer planet goes retrograde and is brightest around <b>opposition</b>, when it is closest to Earth. The time for the same arrangement to repeat (for example, from one opposition to the next) is the <b>synodic period</b>, set by the difference between the two orbital angular speeds. For example, Mars comes to opposition about every 780 days (2 years 2 months).`,
    deep: String.raw`Ptolemy explained retrograde motion with epicycles, and Copernicus explained the same phenomenon much more simply with the heliocentric model. Inner planets go retrograde around inferior conjunction, when they come between Earth and the Sun.`,
    s: ['synodic period: the time for the same arrangement to repeat', 'Earth\'s orbital period (1 year)', 'orbital period of the other planet'],
    r: '1/P is the number of orbits per year. Each time the difference in orbit counts between two planets reaches 1, they return to the same arrangement, so Mars, whose period is close to Earth\'s, has a long synodic period (about 2.1 years).',
    quiz: [
      { q: 'When does an outer planet go retrograde?', opts: ['Around opposition', 'Around conjunction', 'Always'], why: 'It goes retrograde around opposition, when Earth overtakes it on the inside.' },
      { q: 'What do we call a planet moving from west to east among the constellations?', opts: ['Prograde motion', 'Retrograde motion', 'Stationary'], why: 'The usual west-to-east motion is called prograde motion.' },
      { q: 'When does an outer planet look brightest?', opts: ['Around opposition', 'Around conjunction', 'Always the same'], why: 'Around opposition it is closest to Earth and brightest.' },
      { q: 'About every how many days does Mars come to opposition?', unit: 'days', why: 'Mars\'s synodic period is about 780 days (2 years 2 months).' },
    ] },
};
